window.FAQ_INDEX = [{"id": "model-purpose", "title": "What can a price model help us decide?", "theme": "models", "keywords": "forecast trust usefulness validation NPV benchmark", "text": "What can we decide if we cannot predict the exact price? Suppose we buy a share for S 0 = 100 S_0=100 USD/share and plan to sell after T = 1 T=1 year. We want the sale proceeds to beat the same money invested at the benchmark rate g y = 0.02 y r − 1 g_y=0.02\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . We assume a scheduled sale, no dividends or trading costs, and a constant benchmark rate. Discounting the sale price S T S_T and dividing by the purchase cost gives the scaled net present value (NPV): ρ T = − S 0 + S T e − g y T S 0 . \\rho_T=\\frac{-S_0+S_Te^{-g_yT}}{S_0}. The target ρ T > 0 \\rho_T>0 means that the discounted proceeds exceed the purchase cost. Solving this inequality tells us which future prices count as successes: ρ T > 0 ⇔ S T > S 0 e g y T = 100 e 0.02 ≈ 102.02 U S D / s h a r e . \\rho_T>0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad S_T>S_0e^{g_yT}=100e^{0.02}\\approx102.02\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}. Where does the probability come from? Under geometric Brownian motion (GBM), the log price ratio at the sale date is normally distributed. Let μ g = 0.06 y r − 1 \\mu_g=0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} be the mean growth and σ = 0.20 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.20\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} the volatility. The model gives: ln ( S T / S 0 ) = μ g T + σ T Z , Z ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 1 ) . \\ln(S_T/S_0)=\\mu_gT+\\sigma\\sqrt T\\,Z, \\qquad Z\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1). Here Z Z is a standard normal random variable. We substitute the model into the target condition, then subtract the mean and divide by the positive scale σ T \\sigma\\sqrt T : μ g T + σ T Z > g y T , Z > ( g y − μ g ) T σ T = 0.02 − 0.06 0.20 = − 0.20 . \\begin{aligned} \\mu_gT+\\sigma\\sqrt T\\,Z&>g_yT,\\\\ Z&>\\frac{(g_y-\\mu_g)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt T} =\\frac{0.02-0.06}{0.20}=-0.20. \\end{aligned} The normal cumulative distribution function Φ ( z ) \\Phi(z) gives the probability to the left of z z . Our target uses the area to the right, so: ℙ ( ρ T > 0 ) = 1 − Φ ( − 0.20 ) ≈ 0.5793 . \\mathbb P(\\rho_T>0)=1-\\Phi(-0.20)\\approx0.5793. The model gives a 57.9% probability of beating the benchmark and a 42.1% probability of finishing at or below it. These two numbers let us weigh the risk of this particular trade. They describe the scheduled sale date only; selling as soon as the price first reaches a boundary is a different trading rule. How much should we trust the answer? If mean growth were 0.02 y r − 1 0.02\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} instead, the threshold for Z Z would be zero and the success probability would be 50%, so the estimated inputs matter. The model also assumes constant parameters and independent, normally distributed log-price increments. Volatility clustering or heavier tails in the data can change the risk in ways that a better mean estimate would not capture. To check the forecast, we fit the model on earlier observations and record its probabilities for later fixed-horizon trades before their outcomes are known. Over many comparable trades, we compare the forecast probabilities with how often the event actually occurred. For a prediction interval at one date, we check whether the price on that date lies inside it. A band meant to contain an entire path faces a stricter test: every observed price must stay inside its bounds, and a single crossing fails the check. The L5a out-of-sample example develops this distinction. Try it. Keep the parameters above but require a 5% scaled NPV. What changes? Show the answer the price threshold becomes 100 ( 1.05 ) e 0.02 ≈ 107.12 100(1.05)e^{0.02}\\approx107.12 , and the probability falls to about 48.2%. A higher target raises the price threshold and leaves less probability above it. A price model cannot name the future price, but it can give the probability of an outcome we care about. We translate a required gain into a terminal-price threshold, calculate the probability of exceeding it, and examine how the assumptions change that answer.", "url": "questions/model-purpose.html"}, {"id": "model-limits", "title": "Why study models that miss important market behavior?", "theme": "models", "keywords": "limitations heavy tails independent increments out of sample validation", "text": "We use a simple model to connect assumptions with consequences we can calculate and test. A forecast can be a probability distribution over outcomes; it does not have to name the exact future price. The fixed-parameter binomial lattice has only two possible one-step growth rates, and its successive moves are independent. It cannot reproduce heavy-tailed one-step growth or volatility clustering. Constant-parameter GBM permits a continuous range of price movements, but its growth rates are normal and independent, so it also misses those features. Fitting parameters does not make either model memorize the observed path; the fit summarizes the data within the chosen structure. If later observations disagree with the model, we should examine both the parameter uncertainty and the structural assumptions, because the disagreement alone does not tell us which one caused it. A useful next step is to ask a specific question: does the forecast assign reasonable probabilities to later outcomes, under a clearly stated evaluation rule? This is why we separate fitting from out-of-sample testing. Updating the parameters may help, but each updated forecast must be tested on observations that were unavailable when it was made. A simple model connects assumptions with consequences we can calculate and test. Its forecasts become useful when we state the event clearly and compare the forecast probabilities with later outcomes.", "url": "questions/model-limits.html"}, {"id": "growth-and-drift", "title": "How do mean growth and drift differ?", "theme": "growth", "keywords": "mu mu_g μ μ_g half variance correction typical median expected average", "text": "Which averages do drift and mean growth describe? Geometric Brownian motion (GBM) uses the drift μ \\mu to set expected price growth and the volatility σ \\sigma to set the size of the random changes. With constant parameters, the price equation is: d S ( t ) S ( t ) = μ d t + σ d W ( t ) . \\frac{dS(t)}{S(t)}=\\mu\\,dt+\\sigma\\,dW(t). Here W ( t ) W(t) is a Wiener process; its increment over Δ t \\Delta t has variance Δ t \\Delta t . The random price increment has zero conditional mean, so averaging the price equation gives d 𝔼 [ S ( t ) ] / d t = μ 𝔼 [ S ( t ) ] d\\mathbb E[S(t)]/dt=\\mu\\mathbb E[S(t)] , whose solution is 𝔼 [ S T ] = S 0 e μ T \\mathbb E[S_T]=S_0e^{\\mu T} . To see how drift relates to mean growth, we compare this expression with the average we obtain from the log-price solution. At a fixed time T T , the solution written in terms of mean growth μ g \\mu_g is: ln ( S T / S 0 ) = μ g T + σ T Z , Z ∼ 𝒩 ( 0 , 1 ) . \\ln(S_T/S_0)=\\mu_gT+\\sigma\\sqrt T\\,Z, \\qquad Z\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1). When we average this equation, the random term vanishes because 𝔼 [ Z ] = 0 \\mathbb E[Z]=0 , so μ g \\mu_g is the expected log growth per unit time. To average the price itself, we must exponentiate before we average. The normal identity 𝔼 [ e a Z ] = e a 2 / 2 \\mathbb E[e^{aZ}]=e^{a^2/2} , valid for any real number a a , gives: 𝔼 [ S T ] = S 0 e μ g T 𝔼 [ e σ T Z ] = S 0 e ( μ g + σ 2 / 2 ) T . \\mathbb E[S_T] =S_0e^{\\mu_gT}\\mathbb E[e^{\\sigma\\sqrt T Z}] =S_0e^{(\\mu_g+\\sigma^2/2)T}. Comparing this result with 𝔼 [ S T ] = S 0 e μ T \\mathbb E[S_T]=S_0e^{\\mu T} gives μ = μ g + σ 2 / 2 \\mu=\\mu_g+\\sigma^2/2 , or μ g = μ − σ 2 / 2 \\mu_g=\\mu-\\sigma^2/2 . This is the half-variance correction . Averaging prices and averaging their logarithms are different operations, and for GBM the normal identity above gives the exact difference between them. The L4b derivation obtains the same correction from Itô’s lemma. A numerical comparison. Let S 0 = 100 S_0=100 USD/share, μ = 0.08 y r − 1 \\mu=0.08\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , σ = 0.20 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.20\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , and T = 1 T=1 year. Then μ g = 0.06 y r − 1 \\mu_g=0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , giving: 𝔼 [ S T ] = 100 e 0.08 ≈ 108.33 U S D / s h a r e , median ( S T ) = 100 e 0.06 ≈ 106.18 U S D / s h a r e . \\begin{aligned} \\mathbb E[S_T]&=100e^{0.08}\\approx108.33\\ \\mathrm{USD/share},\\\\ \\operatorname{median}(S_T)&=100e^{0.06}\\approx106.18\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}. \\end{aligned} The median splits the possible prices into two equally likely halves. We obtain it by setting Z = 0 Z=0 , the median of the standard normal draw, because the exponential function is increasing. The long right tail pulls the expected price above the median. Neither summary identifies a particular future path. Which number belongs in a simulation? Either one, as long as it appears in the matching form. The two equivalent updates are: S t + Δ t = S t e μ g Δ t + σ Δ t Z = S t e ( μ − σ 2 / 2 ) Δ t + σ Δ t Z . S_{t+\\Delta t}=S_t e^{\\mu_g\\Delta t+\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\,Z} =S_t e^{(\\mu-\\sigma^2/2)\\Delta t+\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\,Z}. The same draw Z Z gives the same price in both forms. If we estimate μ g \\mu_g from observed growth rates, the correction is already included, and subtracting it a second time would make the simulated mean growth too low. Try it. Hold μ = 0.08 y r − 1 \\mu=0.08\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} fixed and increase σ \\sigma to 0.40 y r − 1 / 2 0.40\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} . What happens to the two averages? Show the answer μ g = 0 \\mu_g=0 , the expected one-year price remains about 108.33 USD/share, and the median becomes 100 USD/share. Higher volatility widens the gap between the two averages. Drift is the growth rate of the expected price. Mean growth is the expected log growth per unit time. Under GBM, the half-variance correction connects the two.", "url": "questions/growth-and-drift.html"}, {"id": "growth-return-volatility", "title": "How do growth rate, log return, and volatility differ?", "theme": "growth", "keywords": "sigma σ annualize units 252 sqrt delta t rate return", "text": "All three quantities start from the same observed price changes and express them in different units. For observations separated by Δ t \\Delta t years, the log return r k r_k and growth rate g k g_k are: r k = ln ( S k S k − 1 ) , g k = r k Δ t . r_k=\\ln\\left(\\frac{S_k}{S_{k-1}}\\right), \\qquad g_k=\\frac{r_k}{\\Delta t}. Quantity Meaning Units when time is in years r k r_k Log return over one observation interval Dimensionless g k g_k Log return per unit time y e a r − 1 \\mathrm{year}^{-1} σ g \\sigma_g Sample standard deviation of the observed growth rates y e a r − 1 \\mathrm{year}^{-1} σ r ( Δ t ) \\sigma_r(\\Delta t) Sample standard deviation of interval log returns Dimensionless σ ̂ \\hat\\sigma Estimated GBM volatility parameter y e a r − 1 / 2 \\mathrm{year}^{-1/2} For a fixed observation interval, multiplying every observation by a constant multiplies the standard deviation by the same constant. Therefore: σ r ( Δ t ) = Δ t σ g . \\sigma_r(\\Delta t)=\\Delta t\\,\\sigma_g. Under the constant-parameter GBM model, growth rates have variance σ 2 / Δ t \\sigma^2/\\Delta t . We recover the model’s volatility estimate from: σ ̂ = Δ t σ g = σ r ( Δ t ) Δ t . \\hat\\sigma=\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\,\\sigma_g =\\frac{\\sigma_r(\\Delta t)}{\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}. For daily observations with Δ t = 1 / 252 \\Delta t=1/252 , a growth-rate standard deviation of approximately 3.1749 y e a r − 1 3.1749\\ \\mathrm{year}^{-1} gives σ ̂ ≈ 0.20 y e a r − 1 / 2 \\hat\\sigma\\approx0.20\\ \\mathrm{year}^{-1/2} . Starting instead from the daily log-return standard deviation 0.012599 0.012599 and multiplying by 252 \\sqrt{252} gives the same estimate. The first conversion is an exact change of units. Square-root-of-time scaling is different: using it to forecast risk over another horizon requires assumptions about dependence and variability, and volatility clustering is a reason to check them. The sample mean of the g k g_k estimates μ g \\mu_g . To recover μ ̂ \\hat\\mu , add σ ̂ 2 / 2 \\hat\\sigma^2/2 , not half the unscaled variance of the annualized daily growth rates. They describe a price change, or its variability, in different units. Dividing a log return by the observation interval gives a growth rate, and converting the standard deviation of either one to GBM volatility also uses the time step.", "url": "questions/growth-return-volatility.html"}, {"id": "volatility-purpose", "title": "If volatility cannot predict direction, why is it useful?", "theme": "growth", "keywords": "volatility direction risk clustering useful persistence duration", "text": "Volatility tells us how wide a range of outcomes we should be prepared for. A price distribution with a larger spread gives different probabilities of crossing a loss or profit threshold, even when we cannot determine the sign of the next move. Volatility clustering is the tendency for large movements to occur near other large movements. We observed persistence in growth-rate magnitudes even when signed growth rates had little linear autocorrelation. We conclude that recent large movements can inform our expectation of the size of future movements. That conclusion does not guarantee another large movement, imply that gains and losses alternate, or establish a 50/50 chance of an up move. There is no single duration after which every volatility cluster ends. We can inspect the autocorrelation of magnitudes to describe persistence in a dataset and to evaluate models that update their risk estimates. An autocorrelation plot is not a countdown to the end of the current episode, and the observed pattern alone does not identify what caused it. Volatility describes the range of outcomes we should prepare for. The size of price movements changes the probability of crossing a loss or profit threshold, even when we cannot predict the direction of the next move.", "url": "questions/volatility-purpose.html"}, {"id": "volatility-clustering", "title": "What persists during a volatile period?", "theme": "growth", "keywords": "clustering persistence autocorrelation magnitude signed direction", "text": "What persists when direction is hard to predict? Volatility clustering describes persistence in the size of movements. A turbulent period can contain both gains and losses. We measure direction with signed growth rates and movement size with their absolute values. First we convert consecutive prices into growth rates. For asset i i , we divide the log price ratio by the observation interval Δ t j \\Delta t_j : g j , j − 1 ( i ) = 1 Δ t j ln ( S j ( i ) S j − 1 ( i ) ) . g_{j,j-1}^{(i)}=\\frac{1}{\\Delta t_j}\\ln\\left(\\frac{S_j^{(i)}}{S_{j-1}^{(i)}}\\right). A change from 100 to 101 USD/share over 1 / 252 1/252 year gives a log return ln ( 1.01 ) ≈ 0.00995 \\ln(1.01)\\approx0.00995 and a growth rate 252 ln ( 1.01 ) ≈ 2.507 y r − 1 252\\ln(1.01)\\approx2.507\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The rate is large because we divide a small one-day change by a small fraction of a year. If that same log-price change occurred on each of 252 trading days, the accumulated log change would be 2.507. Here the per-year scale simply records one observed day; a forecast requires a model for the days that follow. The L3a lecture figure: daily SPY and AMD growth rates, a shared high-volatility period, and an isolated AMD price jump. What do we see? The figure comes from the Volatility Clustering section of L3a. The shaded March 2020 period contains many large movements of both signs in SPY and AMD. The April 2016 AMD spike, by contrast, is a single large jump. Clustering refers to a run of large movements, so we distinguish the turbulent period from the isolated spike. The lecture measures persistence using autocorrelation , the linear association between observations separated by a specified number of time steps. Applied to signed growth rates, it asks whether positive or negative deviations tend to recur. Applied to growth magnitudes, it asks whether large or small movements tend to recur. Positive magnitude autocorrelation across several lags is evidence of persistence in movement size. Why does this matter for a model? A model with a fixed volatility applies the same uncertainty scale after calm days and turbulent days. Clustering suggests that the size of recent movements carries information about upcoming risk. When we reach the lattice and GBM models, we can ask whether their assumptions preserve or discard that information. Try it. If signed growth has little autocorrelation but magnitude has persistent positive autocorrelation, can we infer the sign of tomorrow’s change? Show the answer no. The evidence supports persistence in the size of movements. It gives neither a prediction of direction nor a date by which a turbulent episode must end. Volatility clustering is persistence in the size of price movements. Large gains and large losses can occur in the same turbulent period, so evidence of clustering informs our view of risk without telling us the direction of the next move.", "url": "questions/volatility-clustering.html"}, {"id": "forecasting-and-pricing", "title": "Why does one tree use two probabilities?", "theme": "lattices", "keywords": "risk neutral real world p q arbitrage replication pricing forecasting", "text": "Why does one tree use two probabilities? We ask the same tree two different questions. Suppose a share costs 100 USD today and will be worth either 120 or 90 USD after one step, so the up and down factors are u = 1.20 u=1.20 and d = 0.90 d=0.90 . These prices stay fixed for both questions. Forecasting: how often might each outcome occur? Let the estimated real-world up probability be p = 0.65 p=0.65 . Weighting the two prices by their forecast probabilities gives: 𝔼 ℙ [ S 1 ] = 0.65 ( 120 ) + 0.35 ( 90 ) = 109.50 U S D / s h a r e . \\mathbb E_{\\mathbb P}[S_1]=0.65(120)+0.35(90)=109.50\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}. This average summarizes the forecast distribution. It is only as good as the estimated probabilities, which we judge by how well they describe later outcomes. Pricing: what should a specified payoff cost today? Suppose a claim pays H u = 20 H_u=20 USD after an up move and H d = 0 H_d=0 after a down move. Let the risk-free accumulation factor over the same step be R f = 1.02 R_f=1.02 . If trading is frictionless, short positions are allowed, and we can borrow or lend at this rate, the absence of arbitrage requires the risk-neutral weight q q to satisfy: q u + ( 1 − q ) d = R f ⇒ q = R f − d u − d = 1.02 − 0.90 1.20 − 0.90 = 0.40 . qu+(1-q)d=R_f \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q=\\frac{R_f-d}{u-d}=\\frac{1.02-0.90}{1.20-0.90}=0.40. The condition d < R f < u d<R_f<u gives 0 < q < 1 0<q<1 . We use these weights to average the claim’s payoffs and then discount one step: V 0 = q H u + ( 1 − q ) H d R f = 0.40 ( 20 ) + 0.60 ( 0 ) 1.02 ≈ 7.84 U S D . V_0=\\frac{qH_u+(1-q)H_d}{R_f} =\\frac{0.40(20)+0.60(0)}{1.02}\\approx7.84\\ \\mathrm{USD}. Why is that the price? We can build the claim’s payoff from the share and a loan. The claim’s payoff differs by 20 − 0 = 20 20-0=20 USD between the two states, while the share price differs by 120 − 90 = 30 120-90=30 USD/share. Holding 20 / 30 = 2 / 3 20/30=2/3 of a share therefore reproduces the payoff difference. In the down state that holding is worth 60 USD while the claim pays zero, so we pair it with a loan that requires a 60 USD repayment, which means borrowing 60 / 1.02 60/1.02 USD today. After one step the holding is worth 80 or 60 USD and we repay 60 USD, leaving 20 or zero: exactly the claim’s payoff. Building this position costs 100 ( 2 / 3 ) − 60 / 1.02 ≈ 7.84 100(2/3)-60/1.02\\approx7.84 USD today. If the claim traded at any other price, we could trade it against the replicating position for an arbitrage profit under the stated assumptions. Thus p p describes forecast frequencies, while q q values a payoff through a trading argument. Risk-neutral pricing does not assert that the stock is risk-free or that 40% of actual moves will be upward. Try it. Change only p p from 0.65 to 0.50. What changes? Show the answer the forecast expected price becomes 105 USD/share. The replicated payoff still costs about 7.84 USD because the two possible share prices and the borrowing rate are unchanged. The real-world probability describes how often we forecast each move to occur. The risk-neutral weight prices a payoff through a replication argument. The two answer different questions on the same price tree.", "url": "questions/forecasting-and-pricing.html"}, {"id": "calibrate-a-lattice", "title": "How do we construct a lattice from observations?", "theme": "lattices", "keywords": "binomial tree lattice calibration u d p recombining paths nodes", "text": "Our data-driven example estimates the up factor from the observed upward movements, the down factor from the downward movements, and p p from the frequency of up moves. Suppose four consecutive price factors are: 1.02 , 0.99 , 1.04 , 0.98 . 1.02,\\quad0.99,\\quad1.04,\\quad0.98. The estimates are: u = 1.02 + 1.04 2 = 1.03 , d = 0.99 + 0.98 2 = 0.985 , p = 2 4 = 0.50 . u=\\frac{1.02+1.04}{2}=1.03, \\qquad d=\\frac{0.99+0.98}{2}=0.985, \\qquad p=\\frac{2}{4}=0.50. Starting from S 0 = 100 S_0=100 , a two-step lattice has three terminal states: Up moves Terminal price Probability 2 100 u 2 = 106.09 100u^2=106.09 p 2 = 0.25 p^2=0.25 1 100 u d = 101.455 100ud=101.455 2 p ( 1 − p ) = 0.50 2p(1-p)=0.50 0 100 d 2 = 97.0225 100d^2=97.0225 ( 1 − p ) 2 = 0.25 (1-p)^2=0.25 There are four ordered paths but only three terminal states: up–down and down–up reach the same price, so we add their probabilities. The lattice recombines because u d = d u ud=du ; it does not require d = 1 / u d=1/u . The value p = 0.50 p=0.50 comes from this particular sample; the model does not require it. When the observations are stored as growth rates, we recover each price factor with e g k Δ t e^{g_k\\Delta t} , which undoes the earlier logarithm and time scaling. The course estimator averages the factors within the up group and within the down group; averaging the logarithms and then exponentiating would be a different estimator. A zero-change observation joins neither group but still counts in the denominator when we estimate p p . We need at least one up move and one down move to estimate both factors. We estimate the up and down price factors from the observed up and down movements, and the up probability from how often up moves occur. Different paths that reach the same terminal price all contribute to that node’s probability.", "url": "questions/calibrate-a-lattice.html"}, {"id": "lattice-targets", "title": "Which lattice prices beat a trading target?", "theme": "lattices", "keywords": "NPV target strict inequality threshold binomial tail bid ask spread", "text": "How do we turn a trading requirement into a probability? We first find the sale prices that satisfy the requirement, then add the probabilities of those outcomes. In this trade we buy shares now and sell them at a scheduled terminal date, and the scaled NPV compares the discounted sale proceeds with the purchase cost. Which lattice outcomes beat our target? Buying n 0 n_0 shares costs n 0 S 0 n_0S_0 today, and selling them brings in n 0 S T n_0S_T at time T T . Ignoring dividends and trading costs, we discount the proceeds and divide by the purchase cost: ρ T = − n 0 S 0 + n 0 S T e − g y T n 0 S 0 = S T S 0 e − g y T − 1 . \\rho_T=\\frac{-n_0S_0+n_0S_Te^{-g_yT}}{n_0S_0} =\\frac{S_T}{S_0}e^{-g_yT}-1. Try the two-step tree from L3b. Start at 100 USD/share with u = 1.20 u=1.20 , d = 0.90 d=0.90 , and real-world up probability p = 0.65 p=0.65 . Set the benchmark rate to zero for this illustration and require ρ T > 0.05 \\rho_T>0.05 . The terminal prices are 81, 108, and 144 USD/share for zero, one, and two up moves. The sale price must exceed 100 ( 1.05 ) = 105 100(1.05)=105 , so the middle and upper nodes succeed. Their combined probability is: ℙ ( ρ T > 0.05 ) = 2 ( 0.65 ) ( 0.35 ) + 0.65 2 = 0.8775 . \\mathbb P(\\rho_T>0.05)=2(0.65)(0.35)+0.65^2=0.8775. Two paths lead to the middle price but only one leads to the top price, which is why the middle term carries a factor of two. How does the threshold formula reproduce this calculation? At a terminal lattice node with k k up moves, substitute S T / S 0 = u k d N − k S_T/S_0=u^kd^{N-k} . For a target ρ ⋆ > − 1 \\rho_\\star>-1 and factors u > d > 0 u>d>0 , taking logarithms of ρ ( k ) > ρ ⋆ \\rho(k)>\\rho_\\star and collecting the terms in k k gives: k ln u + ( N − k ) ln d > ln ( 1 + ρ ⋆ ) + g y T , k > τ ( ρ ⋆ ) = ln ( 1 + ρ ⋆ ) + g y T − N ln d ln ( u / d ) . \\begin{aligned} k\\ln u+(N-k)\\ln d&>\\ln(1+\\rho_\\star)+g_yT,\\\\ k>\\tau(\\rho_\\star)&=\\frac{\\ln(1+\\rho_\\star)+g_yT-N\\ln d}{\\ln(u/d)}. \\end{aligned} The up count is an integer, so the smallest count that strictly beats the target is ⌊ τ ⌋ + 1 \\lfloor\\tau\\rfloor+1 . We add the binomial probabilities for that count and every higher count. If τ = 2 \\tau=2 exactly, success starts at three up moves, because two up moves only meet the target. Clipping the first count to [ 0 , N + 1 ] [0,N+1] covers the cases where every node succeeds or no node does. This turns a trading requirement into a probability over the model’s terminal outcomes. For the two-step tree, τ = ln ( 1.05 / 0.90 2 ) / ln ( 1.20 / 0.90 ) ≈ 0.902 \\tau=\\ln(1.05/0.90^2)/\\ln(1.20/0.90)\\approx0.902 , so the first successful count is one, as we found by inspecting the prices. Try it. Raise the target to ρ ⋆ = 0.08 \\rho_\\star=0.08 and keep the strict inequality. Which nodes succeed? Show the answer the 108 USD middle node only meets the target, so only the 144 USD node succeeds, giving probability 0.65 2 = 0.4225 0.65^2=0.4225 . Where do bid and ask enter? Buying at an ask of 100.10 and immediately selling at a bid of 99.90 loses 0.20 USD/share. To compute cash flows we could actually trade at, we use the ask price at entry and the bid price at sale. A price model built on midpoints omits that spread cost. First translate the required scaled NPV into a sale-price threshold. Then add the real-world probabilities of the terminal nodes whose prices strictly exceed it.", "url": "questions/lattice-targets.html"}, {"id": "correlated-shocks", "title": "How do independent inputs produce correlated movements?", "theme": "dependence", "keywords": "loading matrix Cholesky square root A C covariance shared shocks normal", "text": "How can independent inputs produce correlated asset movements? We let the assets share a random input. Suppose two assets have volatility parameters 0.20 and 0.30 y r − 1 / 2 0.30\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , with correlation 0.50. The required covariance rate and a loading matrix that produces it are: 𝐂 = [ 0.04 0.03 0.03 0.09 ] y r − 1 , 𝐀 = [ 0.20 0 0.15 0.0675 ] y r − 1 / 2 . \\mathbf C=\\begin{bmatrix}0.04&0.03\\\\0.03&0.09\\end{bmatrix}\\mathrm{yr}^{-1}, \\qquad \\mathbf A=\\begin{bmatrix}0.20&0\\\\0.15&\\sqrt{0.0675}\\end{bmatrix}\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2}. The first diagonal loading is 0.04 = 0.20 \\sqrt{0.04}=0.20 . The shared loading is 0.03 / 0.20 = 0.15 0.03/0.20=0.15 ; the remaining loading is 0.09 − 0.15 2 = 0.0675 ≈ 0.259808 \\sqrt{0.09-0.15^2}=\\sqrt{0.0675}\\approx0.259808 . These choices make 𝐀 𝐀 ⊤ = 𝐂 \\mathbf A\\mathbf A^\\top=\\mathbf C . Let Z 1 Z_1 and Z 2 Z_2 be independent standard normal variables. Write 𝐗 = 𝐀 𝐙 \\mathbf X=\\mathbf A\\mathbf Z for the two noise contributions before time scaling. Expanding the matrix product gives: X 1 = 0.20 Z 1 , X 2 = 0.15 Z 1 + 0.0675 Z 2 . X_1=0.20Z_1,\\qquad X_2=0.15Z_1+\\sqrt{0.0675}\\,Z_2. Both assets use Z 1 Z_1 , which produces their covariance. Independence removes the cross term containing Z 1 Z 2 Z_1Z_2 , and each input has variance one, so: Var ( X 1 ) = 0.20 2 = 0.04 , Var ( X 2 ) = 0.15 2 + 0.0675 = 0.09 , Cov ( X 1 , X 2 ) = 0.20 ( 0.15 ) = 0.03 . \\begin{aligned} \\operatorname{Var}(X_1)&=0.20^2=0.04,\\\\ \\operatorname{Var}(X_2)&=0.15^2+0.0675=0.09,\\\\ \\operatorname{Cov}(X_1,X_2)&=0.20(0.15)=0.03. \\end{aligned} These values have units y r − 1 \\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Dividing the covariance by 0.20 ( 0.30 ) 0.20(0.30) recovers correlation 0.50. One draw illustrates the multiplication: if 𝐙 = ( 1 , − 1 ) ⊤ \\mathbf Z=(1,-1)^\\top , then 𝐗 ≈ ( 0.20 , − 0.109808 ) ⊤ y r − 1 / 2 \\mathbf X\\approx(0.20,-0.109808)^\\top\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} . The two contributions have opposite signs in this draw. Positive correlation describes the joint distribution across many draws; it does not prevent an individual draw from moving the assets in opposite directions. How do these numbers enter a price step? For asset i i , we add the mean log growth and the time-scaled noise in the exponent: S i ( t + Δ t ) = S i ( t ) exp [ μ g , i Δ t + Δ t X i ] , μ g , i = μ i − C i i / 2 . S_i(t+\\Delta t)=S_i(t)\\exp\\!\\left[\\mu_{g,i}\\Delta t+\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\,X_i\\right], \\qquad \\mu_{g,i}=\\mu_i-C_{ii}/2. For Δ t = 1 / 252 \\Delta t=1/252 yr, starting prices of 100 USD/share, and mean growth rates of 0.06 y r − 1 0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} for both assets, the draw above gives next prices of about 101.29 and 99.33 USD/share. All assets share the same random vector within a step, and we draw a fresh vector at the next step. Why use a loading matrix? For independent standard normal inputs, Cov ( 𝐀 𝐙 ) = 𝐀 𝐀 ⊤ \\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf A\\mathbf Z)=\\mathbf A\\mathbf A^\\top . Multiplying the draws by 𝐂 \\mathbf C itself would produce covariance 𝐂 𝐂 ⊤ \\mathbf C\\mathbf C^\\top , which has the wrong values and the wrong units. Try it. Replace the loading 0.15 by − 0.15 -0.15 . What changes? Show the answer both variances stay the same because they use squared loadings. Covariance becomes − 0.03 y r − 1 -0.03\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} and correlation becomes − 0.50 -0.50 . The assets share random inputs through a loading matrix. Multiplying independent standard normal draws by a matrix A produces covariance AAᵀ, so the loadings determine how the assets move together.", "url": "questions/correlated-shocks.html"}, {"id": "covariance-from-data", "title": "How do observations become a covariance matrix?", "theme": "dependence", "keywords": "data matrix G center centered sample covariance Sigma time scaling delta t", "text": "How do observations become the covariance matrix? Suppose we have growth rates for two assets on three aligned dates, collected in the first matrix below. Each row is one date, each column is one asset, and every entry has units y r − 1 \\mathrm{yr}^{-1} : 𝐆 = [ 1 2 2 1 3 3 ] , 𝐠 ′ = [ 2 2 ] , 𝐆 ̃ = [ − 1 0 0 − 1 1 1 ] . \\mathbf G=\\begin{bmatrix}1&2\\\\2&1\\\\3&3\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad \\mathbf g^{\\prime}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\2\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad \\tilde{\\mathbf G}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1&0\\\\0&-1\\\\1&1\\end{bmatrix}. We average each column to obtain its mean (the middle array), then subtract that mean from every observation in the column to obtain the centered matrix on the right. The centered values show whether the assets sit above or below their own means on the same dates. Compute one entry at a time. The first asset’s deviations are ( − 1 , 0 , 1 ) (-1,0,1) , so its sample variance is [ ( − 1 ) 2 + 0 2 + 1 2 ] / ( 3 − 1 ) = 1 y r − 2 [(-1)^2+0^2+1^2]/(3-1)=1\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . For the covariance, we multiply the two assets’ deviations on matching dates, add the products, and divide by the same denominator: [ ( − 1 ) ( 0 ) + ( 0 ) ( − 1 ) + ( 1 ) ( 1 ) ] / 2 = 0.5 y r − 2 . [(-1)(0)+(0)(-1)+(1)(1)]/2=0.5\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}. The matrix product performs all of these calculations at once: 𝚺 ̂ g = 𝐆 ̃ ⊤ 𝐆 ̃ 3 − 1 = 1 2 [ 2 1 1 2 ] = [ 1 0.5 0.5 1 ] y r − 2 . \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_g =\\frac{\\tilde{\\mathbf G}^{\\top}\\tilde{\\mathbf G}}{3-1} =\\frac12\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{bmatrix} =\\begin{bmatrix}1&0.5\\\\0.5&1\\end{bmatrix}\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}. The two variances sit on the diagonal, and the covariance appears in both off-diagonal positions. Both standard deviations equal one, so the correlation is 0.5 / ( 1 ⋅ 1 ) = 0.5 0.5/(1\\cdot1)=0.5 . Which time conversion belongs in GBM? A growth rate is a log return divided by the observation interval, so the time step enters each covariance differently. For daily spacing Δ t = 1 / 252 \\Delta t=1/252 yr, the model’s covariance rate and the interval log-return covariance are: 𝐂 ̂ = 1 252 [ 1 0.5 0.5 1 ] y r − 1 , 𝚺 ̂ r = 1 252 2 [ 1 0.5 0.5 1 ] . \\hat{\\mathbf C}=\\frac1{252}\\begin{bmatrix}1&0.5\\\\0.5&1\\end{bmatrix}\\mathrm{yr}^{-1},\\qquad \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_r=\\frac1{252^2}\\begin{bmatrix}1&0.5\\\\0.5&1\\end{bmatrix}. The GBM step uses Δ t 𝐂 ̂ \\Delta t\\,\\hat{\\mathbf C} as its log-return covariance, which recovers 𝚺 ̂ r \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_r and gives a direct check on the time factors. The growth-rate form of the model has covariance 𝐂 / Δ t \\mathbf C/\\Delta t , which is why 𝐂 ̂ = Δ t 𝚺 ̂ g \\hat{\\mathbf C}=\\Delta t\\,\\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_g . Try it. Add 5 y r − 1 5\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} to every first-asset observation. What happens to the covariance matrix? Show the answer its mean rises from 2 to 7, but its centered deviations remain ( − 1 , 0 , 1 ) (-1,0,1) . Every covariance entry stays the same. Adding a constant changes the level, not the variability about that level. Three observations are enough to make the arithmetic visible, but a useful empirical estimate requires enough aligned data to describe the assets under study. L6a uses the resulting means and covariance to compare allocations. We center each asset’s growth-rate observations, multiply the deviations from matching dates, and average using the sample denominator. The observation interval then converts the growth-rate covariance into the covariance a GBM step needs.", "url": "questions/covariance-from-data.html"}, {"id": "independence", "title": "When do independent single-asset models agree with MA-GBM?", "theme": "dependence", "keywords": "zero correlation independence SA GBM MA GBM joint normal marginal", "text": "Suppose both constructions use the same initial prices, drifts, and individual volatilities. The independent single-asset simulations then have the same joint distribution as the multiple-asset model when all cross-asset covariances are zero. Even when the cross covariances are nonzero, each asset keeps the same individual distribution in either construction; what changes is how the assets move together. The agreement is exact because the model uses jointly normal growth-rate shocks. For a jointly normal vector, a diagonal covariance matrix gives independent components. When the correlations are very weak, independent simulation can be a reasonable approximation in a small example. Whether the difference matters depends on the portfolio, horizon, and number of assets; many small cross terms can add up. Zero correlation does not establish independence for arbitrary data. Suppose X ∈ { − 1 , 0 , 1 } X\\in\\{-1,0,1\\} takes each value with equal probability, and define Y = X 2 Y=X^2 . Their covariance is zero, yet knowing X X determines Y Y exactly. In GBM, the joint-normality assumption supplies the extra structure that turns zero covariance into independence. We should also distinguish equality in distribution from identical simulated paths. Two independent runs can follow different paths even when they use exactly the same model. When the individual parameters match, the two constructions have the same joint distribution if all cross-asset covariances are zero. Zero covariance implies independence here because the model’s shocks are jointly normal.", "url": "questions/independence.html"}, {"id": "fitted-log-price", "title": "What does a fitted log-price line predict?", "theme": "estimation", "keywords": "regression residual fit log price design matrix slope confidence interval", "text": "What does the fitted line predict at one observation? Suppose the fitted intercept is ln ( 100 ) \\ln(100) and the slope is 0.06 y r − 1 0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . At t = 0.5 t=0.5 yr, the fitted log price is ln ( 100 ) + 0.06 ( 0.5 ) \\ln(100)+0.06(0.5) . Exponentiating gives 100 e 0.03 = 103.05 100e^{0.03}=103.05 USD/share. If the observed price is 104 USD/share, the residual is the observed log price minus the fitted log price: r j = ln ( 104 ) − [ ln ( 100 ) + 0.03 ] = 0.00922 . r_j=\\ln(104)-[\\ln(100)+0.03]=0.00922. The residual is dimensionless because we fitted logarithms. In matrix notation, the row [ 1 , 0.5 ] [1,0.5] multiplies the intercept and slope to produce this same prediction. L6b uses the same setup with market growth in place of elapsed time as the predictor. What are we fitting, and what does the interval describe? For observation j j , the lecture fits log price as an intercept plus mean growth times elapsed time. Stacking all observations gives: ln S j = ln S 0 + μ g t j + ϵ j ⇔ 𝐲 = 𝐗 ̂ 𝛉 + 𝛜 . \\ln S_j=\\ln S_0+\\mu_gt_j+\\epsilon_j \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\mathbf y=\\hat{\\mathbf X}\\boldsymbol\\theta+\\boldsymbol\\epsilon. Each row of the design matrix is [ 1 , t j ] [1,t_j] . Least squares chooses the intercept and slope to minimize the sum of squared residuals. For a fitted coefficient θ ̂ j \\hat\\theta_j , the standard error estimates its uncertainty; a smaller value indicates greater precision. The lecture’s two-sided confidence interval is: θ ̂ j ± t 1 − α / 2 , ν SE ( θ ̂ j ) . \\hat\\theta_j\\ \\pm\\ t_{1-\\alpha/2,\\nu}\\operatorname{SE}(\\hat\\theta_j). This interval describes a model coefficient. A prediction interval for a future price must also account for the random price changes still to come. The classical coefficient interval assumes independent, constant-variance, normally distributed errors. Under GBM, log-price errors accumulate through time and share earlier shocks, so the regression-error assumption is a simplification that limits how we can interpret the interval. Fitting a line does not, by itself, validate the uncertainty calculation attached to it. Try it. Is the fitted price of 103.05 USD the expected price at six months under GBM? Show the answer no. It is the fitted log-price trend and, under GBM, the median price. If σ = 0.20 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.20\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , the expected price is 100 e ( 0.06 + 0.20 2 / 2 ) ( 0.5 ) = 104.08 100e^{(0.06+0.20^2/2)(0.5)}=104.08 USD. Averaging prices requires the volatility correction. The fitted line predicts the log price at an observation time. Exponentiating gives a fitted price trend, but reading that trend as the expected price under GBM requires the volatility correction.", "url": "questions/fitted-log-price.html"}, {"id": "ema-updates", "title": "What changes when another price observation arrives?", "theme": "estimation", "keywords": "EMA half life lambda λ update recenter mean variance memory", "text": "What should we do with a new price observation? We convert it to a growth rate, update the estimates using only the information available now, and use the updated estimates for the next forecast. An exponential moving average (EMA) controls how much the new observation changes the estimates. What changes when another price arrives? The new growth observation g k g_k differs from the previous mean by the innovation δ k = g k − m k − 1 \\delta_k=g_k-m_{k-1} . We retain a fraction λ \\lambda of the old information and give the new observation weight 1 − λ 1-\\lambda : m k = λ m k − 1 + ( 1 − λ ) g k = m k − 1 + ( 1 − λ ) δ k . m_k=\\lambda m_{k-1}+(1-\\lambda)g_k =m_{k-1}+(1-\\lambda)\\delta_k. For a 21-observation half-life, λ = 2 − 1 / 21 ≈ 0.9675 \\lambda=2^{-1/21}\\approx0.9675 , so the new observation receives a weight of about 3.25% in the updated mean. The half-life describes how long the estimates remember old data; T T describes how far ahead we forecast. Why is the variance update different? We measure the variance about the updated mean. Re-centering the old observations on the new mean adds the squared shift of the mean to the old variance. Combining that contribution with the new observation’s gives: v k = λ [ v k − 1 + ( m k − 1 − m k ) 2 ] + ( 1 − λ ) ( g k − m k ) 2 . v_k=\\lambda\\left[v_{k-1}+(m_{k-1}-m_k)^2\\right] +(1-\\lambda)(g_k-m_k)^2. The mean update gives m k − m k − 1 = ( 1 − λ ) δ k m_k-m_{k-1}=(1-\\lambda)\\delta_k , and the new observation’s deviation is g k − m k = λ δ k g_k-m_k=\\lambda\\delta_k . Substituting these two deviations and collecting terms yields: v k = λ v k − 1 + λ ( 1 − λ ) 2 δ k 2 + ( 1 − λ ) λ 2 δ k 2 = λ [ v k − 1 + ( 1 − λ ) δ k 2 ] . \\begin{aligned} v_k&=\\lambda v_{k-1}+\\lambda(1-\\lambda)^2\\delta_k^2 +(1-\\lambda)\\lambda^2\\delta_k^2\\\\ &=\\lambda\\left[v_{k-1}+(1-\\lambda)\\delta_k^2\\right]. \\end{aligned} These are exponentially weighted moments, and v k v_k carries no finite-sample correction for bias. We initialize m s = μ ̂ g , 0 m_s=\\hat\\mu_{g,0} and v s = σ ̂ 0 2 / Δ t v_s=\\hat\\sigma_0^2/\\Delta t from the training data. The updated growth variance has units y r − 2 \\mathrm{yr}^{-2} ; multiplying by Δ t \\Delta t and taking the square root gives the GBM volatility σ ̂ k = v k Δ t \\hat\\sigma_k=\\sqrt{v_k\\Delta t} . Each new forecast uses the updated estimates. In the course example, the estimates then stay fixed over that forecast’s horizon, so updating them does not by itself model volatility clusters that occur within the horizon. Work one update. To keep the arithmetic simple, take λ = 0.9 \\lambda=0.9 , previous mean m k − 1 = 0.10 y r − 1 m_{k-1}=0.10\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , previous variance v k − 1 = 4 y r − 2 v_{k-1}=4\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} , and new growth g k = 1.10 y r − 1 g_k=1.10\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Then δ k = 1.00 y r − 1 \\delta_k=1.00\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} and: m k = 0.10 + 0.1 ( 1.00 ) = 0.20 y r − 1 , v k = 0.9 [ 4 + 0.1 ( 1.00 ) 2 ] = 3.69 y r − 2 . m_k=0.10+0.1(1.00)=0.20\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1},\\qquad v_k=0.9[4+0.1(1.00)^2]=3.69\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}. The mean rises. The variance falls because the new observation lies close to the old mean compared with the previous spread. At daily spacing, the updated volatility is 3.69 / 252 = 0.1210 y r − 1 / 2 \\sqrt{3.69/252}=0.1210\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} . Try it. Use g k = 3.10 y r − 1 g_k=3.10\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} instead. What happens to the variance? Show the answer δ k = 3 \\delta_k=3 , m k = 0.40 m_k=0.40 , and v k = 0.9 [ 4 + 0.1 ( 9 ) ] = 4.41 v_k=0.9[4+0.1(9)]=4.41 . A sufficiently large deviation raises the variance. When we evaluate forecasts later, we should use each one as it was saved when issued, so the evaluation reflects only the information available at that time. An exponential moving average updates the estimated mean and variance of the growth rate. Its half-life controls how quickly older observations lose influence, while the forecast horizon sets how far ahead we use the resulting model.", "url": "questions/ema-updates.html"}, {"id": "prediction-bands", "title": "Does a 95% band contain 95% of complete price paths?", "theme": "estimation", "keywords": "95 coverage prediction interval confidence band path whole window out of sample", "text": "A pointwise 95% prediction interval describes the model’s price distribution at one specified future date. Staying inside the interval on every date is a different event. With the fitted GBM parameters held fixed, the bounds at elapsed time τ > 0 \\tau>0 are: L z ( τ ) = S 0 e μ ̂ g τ − z σ ̂ τ , U z ( τ ) = S 0 e μ ̂ g τ + z σ ̂ τ . L_z(\\tau)=S_0e^{\\hat\\mu_g\\tau-z\\hat\\sigma\\sqrt\\tau}, \\qquad U_z(\\tau)=S_0e^{\\hat\\mu_g\\tau+z\\hat\\sigma\\sqrt\\tau}. The values z = 1 z=1 , 1.96 1.96 , and 2.576 2.576 give approximately 68%, 95%, and 99% pointwise coverage under the fitted model. These values are standard normal quantiles, applied to the log price before we exponentiate. The price bounds are not symmetric about the expected price because the price distribution is lognormal. An entire path has many opportunities to leave these bounds. Its prices on different dates are dependent, so we cannot obtain the whole-path probability by simply multiplying the pointwise percentages. These bands also treat the fitted parameters as known; they do not include the uncertainty in those estimates. The lattice example builds its bands from the expectation plus or minus a multiple of the standard deviation and computes its whole-window benchmark directly. Because the lattice distribution is discrete, the familiar normal percentage is not automatically its exact pointwise coverage either. For each example, we should state the band definition and the event being scored. No. A pointwise interval describes the price at one specified date. Staying inside the band on every date is a different event, so whole-path coverage requires its own calculation.", "url": "questions/prediction-bands.html"}, {"id": "simulation-uncertainty", "title": "What uncertainty does a Monte Carlo simulation measure?", "theme": "estimation", "keywords": "Monte Carlo MC standard error SE prediction interval confidence interval simulation paths", "text": "What does a band around simulated wealth describe? The multiple-asset GBM example holds the fitted parameters and initial allocation fixed, simulates correlated prices, and computes wealth on every path. At a given date, the paths give a distribution of possible wealth outcomes under that model. Separate the spread of outcomes from the precision of the mean. We average the wealth values across paths, then measure their spread: W ‾ t = 1 n ∑ k = 1 n W t ( k ) , s W ( t ) = 1 n − 1 ∑ k = 1 n ( W t ( k ) − W ‾ t ) 2 . \\bar W_t=\\frac1n\\sum_{k=1}^{n}W_t^{(k)},\\qquad s_W(t)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}(W_t^{(k)}-\\bar W_t)^2}. Suppose 2,000 independent paths give a mean of 1,100 USD and a standard deviation of 150 USD at a selected date. The example’s approximate 95% pointwise prediction band is: W ‾ t ± 1.96 s W ( t ) = 1 , 100 ± 294 U S D . \\bar W_t\\pm1.96s_W(t)=1{,}100\\pm294\\ \\mathrm{USD}. This band describes the spread of wealth outcomes on that date. The normal approximation behind it can be imperfect because wealth is skewed. By contrast, the Monte Carlo standard error of the mean is 150 / 2 , 000 = 3.35 150/\\sqrt{2{,}000}=3.35 USD, giving an approximate 95% interval of 1 , 100 ± 6.57 1{,}100\\pm6.57 USD for the model’s expected wealth. Pointwise coverage refers to a single date; whole-path coverage is a different event. Estimate a target probability. We discount each path’s terminal wealth to obtain ρ T ( k ) \\rho_T^{(k)} , count the paths strictly above ρ ⋆ \\rho_\\star , and divide by the number of paths: p ̂ = # { k : ρ T ( k ) > ρ ⋆ } n , SE ( p ̂ ) = p ̂ ( 1 − p ̂ ) n . \\hat p=\\frac{\\#\\{k:\\rho_T^{(k)}>\\rho_\\star\\}}{n},\\qquad \\operatorname{SE}(\\hat p)=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}{n}}. If 1,360 of 2,000 paths exceed the target, p ̂ = 0.68 \\hat p=0.68 and its standard error is 0.0104 0.0104 , about one percentage point. An approximate 95% Monte Carlo interval is 0.68 ± 0.0204 0.68\\pm0.0204 . This interval quantifies the effect of using a finite number of random paths with the model inputs held fixed. The simulation first checks that the generated growth rates reproduce the fitted means and covariance. The later comparison with observed 2025 wealth asks a separate question: how well do those fitted inputs describe a new period? Try it. Increase the number of independent paths from 2,000 to 8,000. What should shrink? Show the answer the Monte Carlo standard errors fall by about half. The estimated spread of individual wealth outcomes remains near 150 USD because the model and allocation have not changed. More paths improve numerical precision; uncertainty in the fitted inputs requires a separate analysis. It measures two different things. The spread of simulated wealth describes the range of investment outcomes under the model. The standard error of a simulated mean or probability measures the numerical uncertainty that comes from using a finite number of paths.", "url": "questions/simulation-uncertainty.html"}, {"id": "sim-fit", "title": "What do alpha, beta, and residuals tell us?", "theme": "estimation", "keywords": "SIM single index alpha beta α β regression SVD design matrix R squared", "text": "The single index model (SIM) relates an asset’s growth rate to market growth. The subscript M M means market; the number of portfolio assets is | 𝒫 | \\lvert\\mathcal P\\rvert . The L6b materials use SPY as the market proxy and fit growth rates from aligned trading days. What does an asset’s beta tell us? The single index model separates the fitted market response from the asset-specific residual: g i , t = α i + β i g M , t + ε i , t , β i = Cov ( g i , g M ) Var ( g M ) = ρ i M σ g , i σ g , M . g_{i,t}=\\alpha_i+\\beta_i g_{M,t}+\\varepsilon_{i,t},\\qquad \\beta_i=\\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(g_i,g_M)}{\\operatorname{Var}(g_M)} =\\rho_{iM}\\frac{\\sigma_{g,i}}{\\sigma_{g,M}}. Beta combines the correlation with the ratio of the two growth-rate standard deviations. With correlation 0.60 and an asset standard deviation twice the market’s, beta is 1.20. A 0.10 y r − 1 0.10\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} increase in market growth then raises fitted asset growth by 0.12 y r − 1 0.12\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Suppose α i = 0.02 y r − 1 \\alpha_i=0.02\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} and the observed market growth on a day is 0.10 y r − 1 0.10\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The fitted asset growth is 0.02 + 1.2 ( 0.10 ) = 0.14 y r − 1 0.02+1.2(0.10)=0.14\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . If the observed asset growth is − 0.06 y r − 1 -0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , its fitted residual is − 0.20 y r − 1 -0.20\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The residual records the part of that observation that the line leaves unexplained. How do we fit the line? We choose alpha and beta to minimize the sum of squared residuals. For a full-column-rank design matrix, the lecture gives: 𝛉 ̂ i = arg min 𝛉 ∥ 𝐲 − 𝐗 ̂ 𝛉 ∥ 2 2 = ( 𝐗 ̂ ⊤ 𝐗 ̂ ) − 1 𝐗 ̂ ⊤ 𝐲 . \\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}_i =\\arg\\min_{\\boldsymbol\\theta}\\lVert\\mathbf y-\\hat{\\mathbf X}\\boldsymbol\\theta\\rVert_2^2 =(\\hat{\\mathbf X}^{\\top}\\hat{\\mathbf X})^{-1}\\hat{\\mathbf X}^{\\top}\\mathbf y. Equivalent sample formulas show where the fitted coefficients come from: β ̂ i = Cov ̂ ( g i , g M ) s g , M 2 , α ̂ i = g i ′ − β ̂ i g M ′ . \\hat\\beta_i=\\frac{\\widehat{\\operatorname{Cov}}(g_i,g_M)}{s_{g,M}^2},\\qquad \\hat\\alpha_i=g_i^{\\prime}-\\hat\\beta_i g_M^{\\prime}. The intercept ensures that the fitted line passes through the sample means. The SVD example computes the same estimate as 𝐕 𝚺 − 1 𝐔 ⊤ 𝐲 \\mathbf V\\mathbf\\Sigma^{-1}\\mathbf U^{\\top}\\mathbf y . The estimation example solves the problem three ways, described at the end of this page, and their agreement checks the numerical calculation. How much variation did the fit explain? For an asset with nonzero sample variation, we compare the sum of squared residuals with the sum of squared deviations from the asset’s sample mean: R 2 = 1 − ∥ 𝐫 i ∥ 2 2 ∑ t = 1 N ( g i , t − g i ′ ) 2 . R^2=1-\\frac{\\lVert\\mathbf r_i\\rVert_2^2}{\\sum_{t=1}^{N}(g_{i,t}-g_i^{\\prime})^2}. If the residuals retain 40% of the original sum of squares, then R 2 = 0.60 R^2=0.60 . In this one-predictor regression with an intercept, R 2 R^2 equals the squared sample correlation. It measures in-sample fit; we need later observations to assess how useful the fit is for forecasting. Try it. Suppose the sample correlation is 0.60 and the asset’s sample standard deviation is twice the market’s. What are β ̂ i \\hat\\beta_i and R 2 R^2 ? Show the answer β ̂ i = 1.20 \\hat\\beta_i=1.20 and R 2 = 0.36 R^2=0.36 . Beta measures sensitivity to market growth, while R 2 R^2 measures the fraction of variation explained. The client interview uses both when screening firms. Reading the design matrix. Each row of 𝐗 ̂ \\hat{\\mathbf X} pairs the number one with that day’s market growth. Multiplying by ( α i , β i ) ⊤ (\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)^{\\top} produces the intercept plus the market contribution for each day. The fitted residual vector is what remains after we subtract those fitted values from the observations. The estimation example solves the same least-squares problem three ways: the normal equations, a QR-based solve, and the singular value decomposition (SVD). For the thin SVD 𝐗 ̂ = 𝐔 𝚺 𝐕 ⊤ \\hat{\\mathbf X}=\\mathbf U\\mathbf\\Sigma\\mathbf V^{\\top} , 𝐔 \\mathbf U is N × 2 N\\times2 , and 𝚺 \\mathbf\\Sigma and 𝐕 \\mathbf V are 2 × 2 2\\times2 . Here 𝚺 \\mathbf\\Sigma holds the singular values and should not be confused with 𝚺 g \\mathbf\\Sigma_g , which denotes a growth covariance. Nonzero singular values give full column rank and a unique fit. The single index model separates an asset’s fitted response to market growth from its residual. Alpha is the intercept, beta measures the asset’s sensitivity to market growth, and each residual records the part of an observation that the fitted line leaves unexplained.", "url": "questions/sim-fit.html"}, {"id": "bootstrap-uncertainty", "title": "How uncertain are the fitted alpha and beta?", "theme": "estimation", "keywords": "bootstrap standard error coefficient uncertainty confidence intervals parametric gaussian", "text": "How much might alpha and beta change with another dataset? The fitted line gives one alpha and one beta, and a new set of observations would move that line. A standard error describes the spread we expect in a fitted coefficient across repeated datasets under our error model. It is not the day-to-day spread of asset growth. Where does the standard error come from? Conditional on the observed market growth rates, the classical calculation assumes zero-mean errors with constant variance and no correlation across days. With N > 2 N>2 observations and a full-column-rank design matrix, we estimate the error variance after fitting two coefficients: s g , ε , i 2 = ∥ 𝐫 i ∥ 2 2 N − 2 , Cov ̂ ( 𝛉 ̂ i ) = s g , ε , i 2 ( 𝐗 ̂ ⊤ 𝐗 ̂ ) − 1 . s_{g,\\varepsilon,i}^2=\\frac{\\lVert\\mathbf r_i\\rVert_2^2}{N-2},\\qquad \\widehat{\\operatorname{Cov}}(\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}_i) =s_{g,\\varepsilon,i}^2(\\hat{\\mathbf X}^{\\top}\\hat{\\mathbf X})^{-1}. The square roots of the two diagonal entries give SE ( α ̂ i ) \\operatorname{SE}(\\hat\\alpha_i) and SE ( β ̂ i ) \\operatorname{SE}(\\hat\\beta_i) . Alpha’s standard error has units of inverse years; beta’s is dimensionless. Read an interval. With thousands of observations, the 95% interval is approximately the estimate plus or minus 1.96 standard errors. Suppose α ̂ i = 0.02 y r − 1 \\hat\\alpha_i=0.02\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} with standard error 0.03 y r − 1 0.03\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , and β ̂ i = 1.20 \\hat\\beta_i=1.20 with standard error 0.05 0.05 . Then: α i : 0.02 ± 1.96 ( 0.03 ) = [ − 0.0388 , 0.0788 ] y r − 1 , β i : 1.20 ± 1.96 ( 0.05 ) = [ 1.102 , 1.298 ] . \\begin{aligned} \\alpha_i:&\\quad 0.02\\pm1.96(0.03)=[-0.0388,0.0788]\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1},\\\\ \\beta_i:&\\quad 1.20\\pm1.96(0.05)=[1.102,1.298]. \\end{aligned} The alpha interval includes zero, while the beta interval lies above one. Under the regression assumptions, and relative to the size of each estimate, these data determine the market exposure (beta) more precisely than alpha. The estimation example uses the Student- t t critical value, and the bootstrap comparison uses the large-sample Gaussian value. When the errors are also jointly Gaussian, the classical t t interval is exact. What does the Gaussian bootstrap hold fixed? The uncertainty example keeps 𝐗 ̂ \\hat{\\mathbf X} fixed, draws independent synthetic residuals from a fitted normal distribution, and adds them to the fitted growth: 𝐲 ( k ) = 𝐗 ̂ 𝛉 ̂ i + 𝛆 ( k ) . \\mathbf y^{(k)}=\\hat{\\mathbf X}\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}_i+\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon^{(k)}. For one day, suppose the fitted growth is 0.14 y r − 1 0.14\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} and the synthetic error draw is − 0.20 y r − 1 -0.20\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . That day’s synthetic observation is then − 0.06 y r − 1 -0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . We repeat this addition for every day, keeping each day’s market observation, and fit a new line to the synthetic data. We then repeat the whole process for each of the K = 1 , 000 K=1{,}000 synthetic datasets. The standard deviation of the refitted alphas estimates alpha’s standard error, and likewise for beta. Because the synthetic datasets follow the fitted Gaussian model, the bootstrap should agree with the classical calculation, and that agreement serves as a check. The optional residual-diagnostics example examines heavy tails and dependence across days separately. Try it. Does increasing K K from 1,000 to 4,000 make the original beta estimate twice as precise? Show the answer no. It improves the numerical precision of the bootstrap comparison. The original observations, and the information they carry about beta, are unchanged. A standard error describes how much a coefficient estimate would vary across repeated datasets under an error model. A bootstrap makes that variation visible by constructing synthetic datasets and refitting the line to each one.", "url": "questions/bootstrap-uncertainty.html"}, {"id": "buy-and-hold-weights", "title": "What stays fixed when we buy and hold?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "weights rebalancing Dirichlet simplex allocations concentration buy hold exact log growth", "text": "What stays fixed when we buy and hold? We choose the initial dollar fractions, use them to buy shares, and keep those share counts. The fraction of current wealth in each asset then changes with its price. How do weights become wealth? We allocate the fraction w i w_i of initial wealth W 0 W_0 to asset i i and buy n i = w i W 0 / S i ( 0 ) n_i=w_iW_0/S_i(0) shares. Holding those shares fixed gives: W t = ∑ i n i S i ( t ) = W 0 ∑ i w i S i ( t ) S i ( 0 ) . W_t=\\sum_i n_iS_i(t)=W_0\\sum_iw_i\\frac{S_i(t)}{S_i(0)}. Suppose we split wealth equally between two assets whose prices then change by factors of 1.20 and 0.90. The wealth factor is 0.5 ( 1.20 ) + 0.5 ( 0.90 ) = 1.05 0.5(1.20)+0.5(0.90)=1.05 . The first asset now accounts for 0.5 ( 1.20 ) / 1.05 = 57.14 % 0.5(1.20)/1.05=57.14\\% of wealth and the second for 42.86%. Restoring equal fractions would require selling some of the first asset and buying more of the second. Why do portfolio calculations use a weighted growth rate? Over one short interval, we substitute the asset price factors e g i Δ t e^{g_i\\Delta t} into the wealth ratio: 1 Δ t ln ( W Δ t / W 0 ) = 1 Δ t ln ( ∑ i w i e g i Δ t ) ≈ ∑ i w i g i = g p . \\frac{1}{\\Delta t}\\ln(W_{\\Delta t}/W_0) =\\frac{1}{\\Delta t}\\ln\\left(\\sum_iw_ie^{g_i\\Delta t}\\right) \\approx\\sum_iw_ig_i=g_p. The approximation uses e x ≈ 1 + x e^x\\approx1+x , ln ( 1 + x ) ≈ x \\ln(1+x)\\approx x , and the fact that the weights sum to one. It works well when the changes over the interval are small; the wealth formula gives the exact buy-and-hold result. For the price factors above, the exact portfolio log return is ln ( 1.05 ) = 0.04879 \\ln(1.05)=0.04879 , while the weighted average of the asset log returns is [ ln ( 1.20 ) + ln ( 0.90 ) ] / 2 = 0.03848 [\\ln(1.20)+\\ln(0.90)]/2=0.03848 . The gap shows why we use the exact wealth formula for the investment we actually hold. What does a Dirichlet draw choose? Dirichlet sampling generates candidate long-only weight vectors. Setting every concentration parameter to one samples uniformly over the simplex (the set of nonnegative weights that sum to one), while larger equal concentrations favor weights near an equal allocation. Comparing sampled portfolios lets us explore possible allocations; the optimization in L6a finds the minimum-variance allocation for the specified constraints. Try it. With two assets and 𝛂 = ( 1 , 1 ) \\boldsymbol\\alpha=(1,1) , must every draw give ( 0.5 , 0.5 ) (0.5,0.5) ? Show the answer no. The distribution samples all splits between the two assets uniformly; a draw such as ( 0.8 , 0.2 ) (0.8,0.2) is an 80%/20% investment. Concentration controls which allocations we explore, while the price data determine their estimated reward and risk. The share counts. The initial allocation determines how many shares we buy, and buy-and-hold trading keeps them. The fraction of wealth in each asset then changes as prices move, and the exact wealth calculation follows those fixed share counts.", "url": "questions/buy-and-hold-weights.html"}, {"id": "portfolio-risk", "title": "How do individual growth rates become portfolio risk?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "portfolio variance diversification covariance correlation sigma risk reward", "text": "How do individual growth rates become portfolio reward and risk? We use fixed weights to form the linear growth proxy g p = 𝐰 ⊤ 𝐠 g_p=\\mathbf w^{\\top}\\mathbf g . Its expected value measures reward, and its variance measures risk. The L6a Dirichlet example compares sampled allocations this way; the data example chooses weights by optimization. Estimate the inputs. Each entry of 𝐆 \\mathbf G comes from a pair of consecutive prices on aligned dates: g i , k = ln [ S i ( t k ) / S i ( t k − 1 ) ] / Δ t g_{i,k}=\\ln[S_i(t_k)/S_i(t_{k-1})]/\\Delta t . We average each column to get its mean, then average the products of two columns’ centered observations to get each covariance: g i ′ = 1 N ∑ k = 1 N g i , k , Σ ̂ g , i j = 1 N − 1 ∑ k = 1 N ( g i , k − g i ′ ) ( g j , k − g j ′ ) . g_i^{\\prime}=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{k=1}^{N}g_{i,k},\\qquad \\hat\\Sigma_{g,ij}=\\frac{1}{N-1}\\sum_{k=1}^{N}(g_{i,k}-g_i^{\\prime})(g_{j,k}-g_j^{\\prime}). The diagonal entries are the individual variances. The off-diagonal entries record whether two assets tend to be above or below their own means at the same time. Work through two assets. Suppose the estimated means are ( 0.06 , 0.12 ) y r − 1 (0.06,0.12)\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , their growth-rate standard deviations are ( 2 , 4 ) y r − 1 (2,4)\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , and their correlation is 0.25 0.25 , so the covariance is 0.25 ( 2 ) ( 4 ) = 2 y r − 2 0.25(2)(4)=2\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . For weights ( 0.75 , 0.25 ) (0.75,0.25) : 𝚺 ̂ g = [ 4 2 2 16 ] y r − 2 , 𝔼 ̂ [ g p ] = 0.75 ( 0.06 ) + 0.25 ( 0.12 ) = 0.075 y r − 1 , Var ̂ ( g p ) = 0.75 2 ( 4 ) + 0.25 2 ( 16 ) + 2 ( 0.75 ) ( 0.25 ) ( 2 ) = 4 y r − 2 . \\begin{aligned} \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_g&=\\begin{bmatrix}4&2\\\\2&16\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2},\\\\ \\widehat{\\mathbb E}[g_p]&=0.75(0.06)+0.25(0.12)=0.075\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1},\\\\ \\widehat{\\operatorname{Var}}(g_p)&=0.75^2(4)+0.25^2(16)+2(0.75)(0.25)(2) =4\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}. \\end{aligned} The portfolio standard deviation is 2 y r − 1 2\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . For Δ t = 1 / 252 \\Delta t=1/252 yr, the corresponding diffusion-volatility scale is Δ t σ g , p = 0.126 y r − 1 / 2 \\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\,\\sigma_{g,p}=0.126\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} . The examples use this conversion when comparing growth-rate variability with GBM volatility. Try it. Keep the weights and individual variances fixed, but set the correlation to zero. What changes? Show the answer mean growth stays 0.075 y r − 1 0.075\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , while variance falls to 3.25 y r − 2 3.25\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} and standard deviation to 1.803 y r − 1 1.803\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The smaller covariance reduces portfolio risk. In the course’s linear growth-rate proxy, the weights average the asset means, and the covariance matrix combines the asset variances with their cross terms. Correlation affects portfolio risk through those cross terms.", "url": "questions/portfolio-risk.html"}, {"id": "minimum-variance", "title": "Why mix assets when one has a smaller variance?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "GMV global minimum variance inverse covariance optimization weights short selling", "text": "Why mix assets when one has a smaller variance? Suppose two uncorrelated assets have growth variances 1 and 4 y r − 2 4\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . Put weight w w in the first and 1 − w 1-w in the second. The portfolio variance is: σ g , p 2 = w 2 + 4 ( 1 − w ) 2 = 5 ( w − 0.8 ) 2 + 0.8 [ y r − 2 ] . \\sigma_{g,p}^2=w^2+4(1-w)^2 =5(w-0.8)^2+0.8\\quad[\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}]. The squared term vanishes at w = 0.8 w=0.8 , leaving a variance of 0.8 y r − 2 0.8\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} , which is below that of either asset alone. Each asset’s variance is multiplied by its squared weight, and zero covariance removes the cross term. This calculation finds the best allocation among all possible weights, whereas random sampling compares only the allocations we happen to draw. How do we generalize to many assets? The global minimum-variance (GMV) problem takes the covariance matrix as given and chooses the weights. A positive-definite covariance gives a unique minimum. We first allow signed weights, so the budget is the only constraint: minimize 𝐰 1 2 𝐰 ⊤ 𝚺 g 𝐰 subject to 𝟏 ⊤ 𝐰 = 1 . \\underset{\\mathbf w}{\\operatorname{minimize}}\\quad \\frac12\\mathbf w^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g\\mathbf w \\qquad\\text{subject to}\\quad\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf w=1. Why does the inverse covariance appear? We attach a multiplier to the budget constraint and set the gradient to zero: ℒ ( 𝐰 , λ ) = 1 2 𝐰 ⊤ 𝚺 g 𝐰 − λ ( 𝟏 ⊤ 𝐰 − 1 ) , 𝚺 g 𝐰 = λ 𝟏 ⇒ 𝐰 = λ 𝚺 g − 1 𝟏 . \\begin{aligned} \\mathcal L(\\mathbf w,\\lambda) &=\\tfrac12\\mathbf w^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g\\mathbf w -\\lambda(\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf w-1),\\\\ \\mathbf\\Sigma_g\\mathbf w&=\\lambda\\mathbf1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\mathbf w=\\lambda\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf1. \\end{aligned} The factor 1 / 2 1/2 simplifies differentiation. Requiring the weights to sum to one determines λ \\lambda and gives: 𝐰 G M V = 𝚺 g − 1 𝟏 𝟏 ⊤ 𝚺 g − 1 𝟏 , σ g , G M V 2 = 1 𝟏 ⊤ 𝚺 g − 1 𝟏 . \\mathbf w_{\\mathrm{GMV}} =\\frac{\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf1}{\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf1}, \\qquad \\sigma_{g,\\mathrm{GMV}}^2 =\\frac{1}{\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf1}. The numerator accounts for the variances and covariances, and the denominator rescales the weights so that they sum to one. The data example solves 𝚺 ̂ g 𝐱 = 𝟏 \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_g\\mathbf x=\\mathbf1 and divides 𝐱 \\mathbf x by the sum of its entries. Mean-growth estimates enter only when we add a growth requirement. A two-asset calculation. For the uncorrelated assets above, with growth variances 1 1 and 4 y r − 2 4\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} , the unnormalized vector is ( 1 , 1 / 4 ) y r 2 (1,1/4)\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{2} . Normalizing gives weights ( 0.8 , 0.2 ) (0.8,0.2) and variance 1 / ( 1 + 1 / 4 ) = 0.8 y r − 2 1/(1+1/4)=0.8\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . The lower-variance asset receives more of the budget. What does a negative weight mean? A negative weight is a short position. With 1,000 USD and weights ( 1.2 , − 0.2 ) (1.2,-0.2) , we borrow 200 USD worth of the second asset, sell it, and buy 1,200 USD of the first. The short position is a liability: a price increase raises the cost of buying back the borrowed shares. The lecture’s calculation omits borrowing fees, collateral, and dividends owed. Try it. If an unrestricted GMV solution contains a negative weight, what happens when we require w i ≥ 0 w_i\\geq0 ? Show the answer we solve the problem again over the smaller feasible set. With a positive-definite covariance, the minimum variance increases. If the unrestricted weights were already nonnegative, they would remain optimal. A mix can have less variance than either asset alone, because each asset’s variance is multiplied by its squared weight and the covariance determines the cross terms. The global minimum-variance (GMV) calculation finds the allocation with the least variance under its constraints.", "url": "questions/minimum-variance.html"}, {"id": "growth-targets", "title": "How do exact growth targets differ from growth floors?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "target floor equality inequality efficient frontier GMV mean growth constraints", "text": "How much additional risk does a growth requirement impose? The lecture first requires the weighted sample means to equal a target, ( 𝐠 ′ ) ⊤ 𝐰 = g ⋆ (\\mathbf g^{\\prime})^{\\top}\\mathbf w=g_\\star , while allowing short positions. Plotting the minimum variance at each target traces the minimum-variance frontier. Return to the two uncorrelated assets with variances ( 1 , 4 ) y r − 2 (1,4)\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} and suppose their means are ( 0.04 , 0.14 ) y r − 1 (0.04,0.14)\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The GMV weights ( 0.8 , 0.2 ) (0.8,0.2) give mean growth 0.06 y r − 1 0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . A floor of 0.05 0.05 leaves this allocation unchanged. An exact target of 0.05 0.05 instead requires weights ( 0.9 , 0.1 ) (0.9,0.1) and variance 0.85 y r − 2 0.85\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . The exact target selects a lower-growth, higher-risk allocation than the GMV portfolio; the floor accepts the GMV allocation. To see where those weights come from, write the first-asset weight as w w and the second as 1 − w 1-w . The mean growth is 0.04 w + 0.14 ( 1 − w ) = 0.14 − 0.10 w 0.04w+0.14(1-w)=0.14-0.10w . Setting it to 0.05 gives w = 0.9 w=0.9 . A floor asks only for a mean of at least 0.05; the GMV mean of 0.06 already qualifies. What changes with a floor and long-only weights? We solve the following problem numerically using estimated inputs: minimize 𝐰 𝐰 ⊤ 𝚺 ̂ g 𝐰 subject to ( 𝐠 ′ ) ⊤ 𝐰 ≥ g ⋆ , 𝟏 ⊤ 𝐰 = 1 , 0 ≤ w i ≤ 1 . \\begin{aligned} \\underset{\\mathbf w}{\\operatorname{minimize}}\\quad&\\mathbf w^{\\top}\\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_g\\mathbf w\\\\ \\text{subject to}\\quad &(\\mathbf g^{\\prime})^{\\top}\\mathbf w\\geq g_\\star,\\qquad \\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf w=1,\\qquad 0\\leq w_i\\leq1. \\end{aligned} Try it. Raise the floor to 0.09 y r − 1 0.09\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . What happens? Show the answer the floor now binds, at weights ( 0.5 , 0.5 ) (0.5,0.5) , giving variance 1.25 y r − 2 1.25\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . A long-only floor above 0.14 y r − 1 0.14\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} is infeasible. Setting the floor to the smallest asset mean, as the examples do in their GMV solve, admits every fully invested long-only allocation. The formula for an exact target The two-asset calculation above determines the weights from the budget and growth equations. With more assets, many allocations can satisfy both equations; the covariance determines which has the least risk. The lecture writes that solution using four shorthand coefficients. For a positive-definite 𝚺 g \\mathbf\\Sigma_g and unequal asset sample means, calculate: a = 𝟏 ⊤ 𝚺 g − 1 𝟏 , b = 𝟏 ⊤ 𝚺 g − 1 𝐠 ′ , c = ( 𝐠 ′ ) ⊤ 𝚺 g − 1 𝐠 ′ , d = a c − b 2 > 0 . \\begin{aligned} a&=\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf1,& b&=\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf g^{\\prime},\\\\ c&=(\\mathbf g^{\\prime})^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf g^{\\prime},&d&=ac-b^2>0. \\end{aligned} The budget and growth constraints determine how much of each of two vectors enters the solution. The resulting weights and variance are: 𝐰 ⋆ ( g ⋆ ) = c − b g ⋆ d 𝚺 g − 1 𝟏 + a g ⋆ − b d 𝚺 g − 1 𝐠 ′ , σ g 2 ( g ⋆ ) = a g ⋆ 2 − 2 b g ⋆ + c d = 1 a + a d ( g ⋆ − b a ) 2 . \\begin{aligned} \\mathbf w_\\star(g_\\star) &=\\frac{c-bg_\\star}{d}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf1 +\\frac{ag_\\star-b}{d}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf g^{\\prime},\\\\ \\sigma_g^2(g_\\star) &=\\frac{ag_\\star^2-2bg_\\star+c}{d} =\\frac1a+\\frac ad\\left(g_\\star-\\frac ba\\right)^2. \\end{aligned} The last expression separates the GMV variance, 1 / a 1/a , from the extra variance that the target imposes. The GMV mean is b / a b/a . The upper branch, g ⋆ ≥ b / a g_\\star\\geq b/a , is the efficient one: every point on the lower branch has a counterpart on the upper branch with the same risk and higher growth. An exact target requires the portfolio’s mean growth to equal a chosen value. A floor accepts any allocation that meets or exceeds it, so a floor can keep the minimum-variance portfolio when that portfolio’s mean already clears the floor.", "url": "questions/growth-targets.html"}, {"id": "gbm-target-probability", "title": "How does an NPV target become a normal probability?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "NPV target probability normal Phi terminal first passage threshold benchmark", "text": "We first specify the trade. We buy at S 0 S_0 and sell at the scheduled time T T , ignoring dividends, fees, and other trading costs. With a constant benchmark growth rate g y g_y , the scaled NPV is: ρ T = e − g y T S T S 0 − 1 . \\rho_T=e^{-g_yT}\\frac{S_T}{S_0}-1. The benchmark need not be risk-free; it represents the growth requirement used in this comparison. We ask whether ρ T \\rho_T exceeds a target ρ ⋆ > − 1 \\rho_\\star>-1 . We substitute the GBM solution, add one to both sides, and take logarithms. The event becomes: ( μ g − g y ) T + σ T Z > ln ( 1 + ρ ⋆ ) . (\\mu_g-g_y)T+\\sigma\\sqrt T\\,Z>\\ln(1+\\rho_\\star). For T > 0 T>0 and σ > 0 \\sigma>0 , we divide by the positive quantity σ T \\sigma\\sqrt T . This gives a threshold for the standard normal variable: Z > z ⋆ , z ⋆ = ln ( 1 + ρ ⋆ ) − ( μ g − g y ) T σ T , ℙ ( ρ T > ρ ⋆ ) = 1 − Φ ( z ⋆ ) . Z>z_\\star, \\qquad z_\\star=\\frac{\\ln(1+\\rho_\\star)-(\\mu_g-g_y)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt T}, \\qquad \\mathbb P(\\rho_T>\\rho_\\star)=1-\\Phi(z_\\star). For example, take μ g = 0.06 \\mu_g=0.06 , g y = 0.02 g_y=0.02 , σ = 0.20 \\sigma=0.20 , and T = 1 T=1 in the corresponding year-based units. With target ρ ⋆ = 0 \\rho_\\star=0 , the threshold is z ⋆ = − 0.20 z_\\star=-0.20 , giving a probability of about 0.5793 0.5793 . This is the model’s probability of beating the benchmark at the scheduled sale time. A lower threshold leaves more of the normal distribution to its right. This is why increasing mean growth, with the other quantities fixed, raises the probability even though it lowers z ⋆ z_\\star . This calculation does not give the probability of reaching the target at some earlier time and selling immediately. That is a first-passage question about a different event. We translate the NPV target into a price threshold, substitute the GBM price equation, and standardize. The probability to the right of the resulting normal threshold is the model’s probability of exceeding the target at the scheduled sale date.", "url": "questions/gbm-target-probability.html"}, {"id": "daily-leverage", "title": "Why does daily leverage not multiply the long-run return?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "ETF daily leverage leveraged compounding reset 2x volatility drag", "text": "A daily multiple is applied to each day’s return, and those results compound. Many leveraged ETFs target a multiple of daily performance; over longer horizons, their return can differ substantially from that multiple of the benchmark’s return. See the SEC’s investor bulletin on leveraged and inverse ETFs . Consider an idealized product that delivers exactly twice each day’s benchmark return, ignoring fees, financing costs, and tracking differences. Start both the benchmark and the product at 100. The benchmark gains 10% on day one, reaching 110, then loses 1 / 11 ≈ 9.09 % 1/11\\approx9.09\\% on day two, returning to 100. The two-times daily product follows a different path: 100 ( 1 + 2 ( 0.10 ) ) ( 1 + 2 ( − 1 11 ) ) = 120 ( 9 11 ) ≈ 98.18 . 100(1+2(0.10))\\left(1+2\\left(-\\frac{1}{11}\\right)\\right) =120\\left(\\frac{9}{11}\\right) \\approx98.18. The benchmark finishes unchanged while the product loses approximately 1.82%. This does not mean that every leveraged path must decline. It shows that the sequence of returns and the reset interval matter: when the benchmark returns to its starting price, the leveraged product need not return to its starting value. The multiple applies to each day’s return, and the daily results then compound. A benchmark can return to its starting price while a product that targets twice its daily return finishes below where it started.", "url": "questions/daily-leverage.html"}, {"id": "wealth-and-npv", "title": "How do portfolio weights become wealth and NPV?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "wealth NPV shares budget terminal target scaled discounted benchmark", "text": "How do we evaluate the investment after choosing its weights? We convert the initial dollar allocations to shares, keep the share counts fixed, and value the shares at later prices. The data and multiple-asset GBM examples use the same wealth calculation on observed and simulated prices. Work from dollars to shares. Suppose W 0 = 1 , 000 W_0=1{,}000 USD, weights are ( 0.6 , 0.4 ) (0.6,0.4) , and initial share prices are ( 100 , 50 ) (100,50) USD. We buy six shares of the first asset and eight of the second. In symbols: n i = w i W 0 S i ( 0 ) , W t = ∑ i n i S i ( t ) = W 0 ∑ i w i S i ( t ) S i ( 0 ) . n_i=\\frac{w_iW_0}{S_i(0)},\\qquad W_t=\\sum_i n_iS_i(t)=W_0\\sum_i w_i\\frac{S_i(t)}{S_i(0)}. If the prices after one year are ( 110 , 45 ) (110,45) USD, wealth is 6 ( 110 ) + 8 ( 45 ) = 1 , 020 6(110)+8(45)=1{,}020 USD. The first asset now represents 660 / 1 , 020 = 64.7 % 660/1{,}020=64.7\\% of wealth. Its initial fraction was 60%: the share count stayed at six while its fraction of wealth changed. Did we exceed the benchmark? We discount terminal wealth before comparing it with the initial investment: NPV ( g y , T ) = − W 0 + W T e − g y T , ρ T = W T W 0 e − g y T − 1 . \\operatorname{NPV}(g_y,T)=-W_0+W_Te^{-g_yT},\\qquad \\rho_T=\\frac{W_T}{W_0}e^{-g_yT}-1. For g y = 0.03 y r − 1 g_y=0.03\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} and T = 1 T=1 yr, the scaled NPV is 1.02 e − 0.03 − 1 ≈ − 0.01015 1.02e^{-0.03}-1\\approx-0.01015 . Wealth rose by 20 USD, but its discounted value fell short of the initial investment by about 10.15 USD. To exceed a scaled target ρ ⋆ > − 1 \\rho_\\star>-1 , terminal wealth must satisfy: W T > W 0 ( 1 + ρ ⋆ ) e g y T . W_T>W_0(1+\\rho_\\star)e^{g_yT}. For a zero target, the required wealth is 1 , 030.45 1{,}030.45 USD. This translates the NPV question into an event defined on terminal wealth, which we can count across simulated paths. How do we use the testing data? The data example estimates the inputs from 2014 to 2024 and evaluates both GMV allocations, equal weights, and SPY on 2025 prices. Realized mean growth is ln ( W T / W 0 ) / T \\ln(W_T/W_0)/T when wealth remains positive. Realized risk is the sample standard deviation of daily wealth growth rates. These quantities describe the investment we actually held, whose weights can drift over time. Try it. If we double W 0 W_0 and keep the weights and price ratios fixed, what changes? Show the answer share counts, wealth, and dollar NPV double. Scaled NPV and the probability of exceeding a scaled target stay the same. The calculation allows fractional shares and omits trading costs and dividends. We use the initial dollar allocation and the initial prices to determine the share counts, then value those shares at later prices. NPV discounts the terminal wealth before comparing it with the initial investment.", "url": "questions/wealth-and-npv.html"}, {"id": "sim-risk", "title": "Which covariance does the single index model keep or omit?", "theme": "dependence", "keywords": "SIM residual covariance diagonal idiosyncratic market risk diversification beta", "text": "The SIM covariance separates a common market contribution from asset-specific residual variances. Its diagonal residual matrix sets the residual covariance between different assets to zero. This is an assumption added on top of fitting each asset by least squares. How does the market model supply portfolio inputs? Assume that the residuals have zero mean, are uncorrelated with market growth, and are uncorrelated across assets. Taking means and covariances of the model then gives: 𝛍 g = 𝛂 + 𝛃 μ g , M , 𝚺 g = σ g , M 2 𝛃 𝛃 ⊤ + 𝐃 g . \\boldsymbol\\mu_g=\\boldsymbol\\alpha+\\boldsymbol\\beta\\mu_{g,M},\\qquad \\mathbf\\Sigma_g=\\sigma_{g,M}^2\\boldsymbol\\beta\\boldsymbol\\beta^{\\top}+\\mathbf D_g. Substituting estimates from the same training observations gives: 𝐠 ̂ S I M = 𝛂 ̂ + 𝛃 ̂ g M ′ , 𝚺 ̂ g , S I M = s g , M 2 𝛃 ̂ 𝛃 ̂ ⊤ + 𝐃 ̂ g . \\hat{\\mathbf g}_{\\mathrm{SIM}}=\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\alpha}+\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}g_M^{\\prime},\\qquad \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_{g,\\mathrm{SIM}}=s_{g,M}^2\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\beta}^{\\top}+\\hat{\\mathbf D}_g. The diagonal entries contain both market and residual variance, while the off-diagonal entries contain only the shared market contribution. Positive residual variances make the fitted covariance positive definite. These inputs replace the empirical ones in L6a’s allocation problem. Which input changes when we use the SIM? Only the covariance. With an intercept and the same training days, α ̂ i = g i ′ − β ̂ i g M ′ \\hat\\alpha_i=g_i^{\\prime}-\\hat\\beta_i g_M^{\\prime} , so 𝐠 ̂ S I M = 𝐠 ′ \\hat{\\mathbf g}_{\\mathrm{SIM}}=\\mathbf g^{\\prime} and the reward inputs agree. The covariance changes because the model removes residual co-movement. For two different assets, the sample covariance splits exactly into a part the SIM keeps and a part it omits: Σ ̂ g , i j = β ̂ i β ̂ j s g , M 2 ⏟ SIM keeps + Cov ̂ ( 𝐫 i , 𝐫 j ) ⏟ SIM omits , i ≠ j . \\hat\\Sigma_{g,ij} =\\underbrace{\\hat\\beta_i\\hat\\beta_j s_{g,M}^2}_{\\text{SIM keeps}} +\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\operatorname{Cov}}(\\mathbf r_i,\\mathbf r_j)}_{\\text{SIM omits}},\\qquad i\\ne j. On the diagonal, the SIM divides residual sums of squares by N − 2 N-2 , whereas the sample residual covariance uses N − 1 N-1 . Thus, the SIM residual contribution is larger by the factor ( N − 1 ) / ( N − 2 ) (N-1)/(N-2) , which is nearly one with thousands of observations. Build a two-asset covariance. Suppose the fitted betas are ( 1 , 1.5 ) (1,1.5) , the market growth variance is 4 y r − 2 4\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} , and fitted residual variances are ( 1 , 4 ) y r − 2 (1,4)\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . Then: 𝚺 ̂ g , S I M = 4 [ 1 1.5 1.5 2.25 ] + [ 1 0 0 4 ] = [ 5 6 6 13 ] y r − 2 . \\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_{g,\\mathrm{SIM}} =4\\begin{bmatrix}1&1.5\\\\1.5&2.25\\end{bmatrix} +\\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\\\0&4\\end{bmatrix} =\\begin{bmatrix}5&6\\\\6&13\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}. For fixed weights, the portfolio beta is β p = 𝐰 ⊤ 𝛃 \\beta_p=\\mathbf w^{\\top}\\boldsymbol\\beta . Multiplying the covariance by the weight vector on both sides gives the general risk decomposition: Var ( g p ) = β p 2 σ g , M 2 + ∑ i w i 2 σ g , ε , i 2 . \\operatorname{Var}(g_p)=\\beta_p^2\\sigma_{g,M}^2+\\sum_i w_i^2\\sigma_{g,\\varepsilon,i}^2. At equal weights, the fitted portfolio beta is 0.5 ( 1 ) + 0.5 ( 1.5 ) = 1.25 0.5(1)+0.5(1.5)=1.25 . Substituting the fitted inputs gives: Var ̂ S I M ( g p ) = 1.25 2 ( 4 ) ⏟ 6.25 market + 0.5 2 ( 1 ) + 0.5 2 ( 4 ) ⏟ 1.25 residual = 7.5 y r − 2 . \\widehat{\\operatorname{Var}}_{\\mathrm{SIM}}(g_p) =\\underbrace{1.25^2(4)}_{6.25\\text{ market}} +\\underbrace{0.5^2(1)+0.5^2(4)}_{1.25\\text{ residual}} =7.5\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2}. If the residual covariance were instead 1 y r − 2 1\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} , with the same diagonal entries, it would add the cross term 2 ( 0.5 ) ( 0.5 ) ( 1 ) = 0.5 y r − 2 2(0.5)(0.5)(1)=0.5\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} and the variance would be 8 y r − 2 8\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . Positive residual co-movement increases the risk of these positive-weight holdings. Which risk can diversification reduce? Residual risk. With | 𝒫 | \\lvert\\mathcal P\\rvert equally weighted assets and uncorrelated residuals of common variance v v , the residual risk is v / | 𝒫 | v/\\lvert\\mathcal P\\rvert . More generally, if every residual variance is at most σ ‾ 2 \\bar\\sigma^2 , the residual risk is at most σ ‾ 2 / | 𝒫 | \\bar\\sigma^2/\\lvert\\mathcal P\\rvert . Market risk remains β p 2 σ g , M 2 \\beta_p^2\\sigma_{g,M}^2 , determined by the portfolio beta. How do the portfolio examples compare allocations? The risky-assets example scores both weight vectors, the data-optimal weights and the SIM weights, under the sample covariance at common growth floors, then tests both on 2025 prices. At the same floor, the data-optimal weights can have no more sample-covariance risk than the SIM weights. Scoring both under one covariance separates the effect of choosing different holdings from the effect of measuring risk differently. Try it. In the two-asset calculation, replace residual covariance 1 1 by − 1 y r − 2 -1\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . Show the answer with the same diagonal entries, the variance becomes 7 y r − 2 7\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-2} . The SIM now overstates this portfolio’s risk by omitting a negative covariance. The single index model (SIM) keeps the covariance that comes from shared market exposure. Its diagonal residual approximation omits the covariance between different assets’ residuals, which can make the estimated portfolio risk higher or lower than the sample-covariance result.", "url": "questions/sim-risk.html"}, {"id": "risk-free-allocation", "title": "What changes when we lend or borrow?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "capital allocation line CAL risk free lending borrowing leverage Sharpe ratio", "text": "We combine a risky portfolio with an asset whose growth rate is known over the holding period. The weight on the risk-free asset determines whether we lend or borrow. Here we use the course’s fixed-weight, linear growth-rate proxy; the buy-and-hold wealth calculation instead follows the actual holdings as their wealth fractions change. What happens when we lend or borrow? Put fraction w f w_f in the risk-free asset and fraction 1 − w f 1-w_f in a fixed risky portfolio. The risk-free asset’s known growth contributes to the mean and adds no variance: 𝔼 [ g c ] = g f + ( 1 − w f ) ( 𝔼 [ g p ] − g f ) , σ g , c = | 1 − w f | σ g , p . \\mathbb E[g_c]=g_f+(1-w_f)(\\mathbb E[g_p]-g_f),\\qquad \\sigma_{g,c}=|1-w_f|\\sigma_{g,p}. For daily observations ( Δ t = 1 / 252 \\Delta t=1/252 yr), suppose g f = 0.02 y r − 1 g_f=0.02\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , 𝔼 [ g p ] = 0.08 y r − 1 \\mathbb E[g_p]=0.08\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , and σ g , p = 3.0 y r − 1 \\sigma_{g,p}=3.0\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . A 50% risk-free allocation gives mean growth 0.05 0.05 and standard deviation 1.5 y r − 1 1.5\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Borrowing 50% of initial wealth means w f = − 0.5 w_f=-0.5 : mean growth becomes 0.11 0.11 and the standard deviation 4.5 y r − 1 4.5\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Borrowing increases both the risky exposure and the uncertainty. For w f ≤ 1 w_f\\leq1 , eliminating the risky fraction gives the capital allocation line (CAL): 𝔼 [ g c ] = g f + S R p σ g , c . \\mathbb E[g_c]=g_f+\\mathrm{SR}_p\\,\\sigma_{g,c}. Its slope is the risky portfolio’s Sharpe ratio, ( 0.08 − 0.02 ) / 3 = 0.02 (0.08-0.02)/3=0.02 . Under independent-increment scaling, the annualized ratio is 252 ( 0.02 ) = 0.3175 \\sqrt{252}(0.02)=0.3175 . Expressing daily growth in inverse years does not, by itself, annualize this ratio. Try it. For a target mean growth of 0.065 y r − 1 0.065\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , what risk-free fraction is needed? Show the answer 1 − w f = ( 0.065 − 0.02 ) / ( 0.08 − 0.02 ) = 0.75 1-w_f=(0.065-0.02)/(0.08-0.02)=0.75 , so w f = 0.25 w_f=0.25 and the standard deviation is 2.25 y r − 1 2.25\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The risk-free fraction controls exposure to the risky portfolio. Lending reduces that exposure, and borrowing increases it. When the risky portfolio has positive excess mean growth, borrowing raises both expected growth and risk.", "url": "questions/risk-free-allocation.html"}, {"id": "tangent-portfolio", "title": "Which weights describe the risky fund and the whole investment?", "theme": "portfolios", "keywords": "tangent tangency normalized weights complete investment risk free Sharpe", "text": "Which weights describe the risky fund, and which describe the whole investment? With 1,000 USD of initial wealth, risky weights of ( 0.30 , 0.45 ) (0.30,0.45) put 300 USD in the first firm and 450 USD in the second, and the remaining 250 USD is lent at the risk-free rate. The risky weights sum to 0.75 0.75 , so w f = 0.25 w_f=0.25 and the normalized risky-fund weights are ( 0.4 , 0.6 ) (0.4,0.6) . If we instead borrowed 50% of initial wealth, the complete-portfolio risky weights would be ( 0.6 , 0.9 ) (0.6,0.9) , with the same normalized mix. Which risky portfolio defines the steepest capital allocation line? The tangent portfolio maximizes excess mean growth per unit standard deviation. When the covariance is positive definite, signed risky weights are allowed, and the GMV mean growth exceeds g f g_f , the lecture obtains: 𝐰 𝒯 = 𝚺 g − 1 𝐞 𝟏 ⊤ 𝚺 g − 1 𝐞 , 𝐞 = 𝛍 g − g f 𝟏 . \\mathbf w_{\\mathcal T}=\\frac{\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf e}{\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf\\Sigma_g^{-1}\\mathbf e}, \\qquad \\mathbf e=\\boldsymbol\\mu_g-g_f\\mathbf1. The inverse covariance adjusts the excess-growth vector for risk; the denominator makes the risky fund’s weights sum to one. Long-only restrictions require a numerical solve. The lecture’s problem (SIM-2) measures the risky weights as fractions of total initial wealth: minimize 𝐰 𝐰 ⊤ 𝚺 ̂ g , S I M 𝐰 subject to ( 𝐠 ̂ S I M − g f 𝟏 ) ⊤ 𝐰 ≥ g ⋆ − g f , 0 ≤ w i ≤ 1 , w f = 1 − 𝟏 ⊤ 𝐰 . \\begin{aligned} \\underset{\\mathbf w}{\\operatorname{minimize}}\\quad &\\mathbf w^{\\top}\\hat{\\mathbf\\Sigma}_{g,\\mathrm{SIM}}\\mathbf w\\\\ \\text{subject to}\\quad &(\\hat{\\mathbf g}_{\\mathrm{SIM}}-g_f\\mathbf1)^{\\top}\\mathbf w\\geq g_\\star-g_f,\\\\ &0\\leq w_i\\leq1,\\qquad w_f=1-\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf w. \\end{aligned} The risky weights can sum to more than one because borrowing is allowed. The bounds w i ≤ 1 w_i\\leq1 cap each risky position at the initial wealth, so they eventually limit how far we can borrow along a fixed risky mix. Recover the risky fund. For feasible targets g ⋆ > g f g_\\star>g_f , and until an upper bound binds, we normalize a nonzero solution by its risky fraction. Every such target selects the same long-only tangent mix: 𝐰 𝒯 = 𝐰 𝟏 ⊤ 𝐰 , 𝐰 ( g ⋆ ) = ( 1 − w f ) 𝐰 𝒯 , 1 − w f = g ⋆ − g f 𝐠 ̂ S I M ⊤ 𝐰 𝒯 − g f . \\mathbf w_{\\mathcal T}=\\frac{\\mathbf w}{\\mathbf1^{\\top}\\mathbf w},\\qquad \\mathbf w(g_\\star)=(1-w_f)\\mathbf w_{\\mathcal T},\\qquad 1-w_f=\\frac{g_\\star-g_f}{\\hat{\\mathbf g}_{\\mathrm{SIM}}^{\\top}\\mathbf w_{\\mathcal T}-g_f}. Follow the complete investment. The risky/risk-free example fixes the initial share counts and lets the risk-free position accumulate. With the tangent weights normalized to sum to one, wealth is: W t = W 0 [ w f e g f t + ( 1 − w f ) ∑ i w 𝒯 , i S i ( t ) S i ( 0 ) ] . W_t=W_0\\left[w_fe^{g_ft}+(1-w_f)\\sum_i w_{\\mathcal T,i}\\frac{S_i(t)}{S_i(0)}\\right]. When w f < 0 w_f<0 , the first term is a debt that grows over time. The example assumes one rate for both borrowing and lending. Under buy-and-hold, the risk-free and firm fractions drift, so the realized growth and risk need not equal the fixed-weight CAL predictions. The comparison uses 2025 prices that were held out of estimation and scores both tangent funds under the same sample covariance. Try it. Let W 0 = 1 , 000 W_0=1{,}000 USD, w f = − 0.5 w_f=-0.5 , g f = 0.02 y r − 1 g_f=0.02\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , and suppose the risky fund gains 10% over one year. What is net wealth? Show the answer net wealth is 1 , 000 [ − 0.5 e 0.02 + 1.5 ( 1.10 ) ] = 1 , 139.90 1{,}000[-0.5e^{0.02}+1.5(1.10)]=1{,}139.90 USD. The 1,650 USD risky holding is partly offset by a 510.10 USD repayment obligation. The normalized risky weights describe the composition of the risky fund. The complete-investment weights also specify how much wealth is lent or borrowed at the risk-free rate.", "url": "questions/tangent-portfolio.html"}, {"id": "discounting", "title": "Why discount cash flows, and what does positive NPV mean?", "theme": "cashflows", "keywords": "discount present value NPV cash flow compounding benchmark", "text": "Why is receiving 110 USD next year worth less than receiving it today? Suppose our benchmark pays 5% with annual compounding. An investment of 100 USD today becomes 105 USD in one year. To find what a promised 110 USD is worth today, we reverse that calculation: 110 / 1.05 = 104.76 110/1.05=104.76 USD. Discounting asks how much we would need to invest today, at the benchmark rate, to reach the future payment. How do the cash-flow symbols become a value? At date j j , we multiply each cash amount c j , k c_{j,k} by its direction ν j , k \\nu_{j,k} (positive for a receipt, negative for a payment) and add the results. We then discount that date’s net cash flow to time zero: c ‾ j = ∑ k = 1 m j c j , k ν j , k , NPV 0 = ∑ j = 0 N 𝒟 j , 0 − 1 c ‾ j . \\bar c_j=\\sum_{k=1}^{m_j}c_{j,k}\\nu_{j,k},\\qquad \\operatorname{NPV}_0=\\sum_{j=0}^{N}\\mathcal D_{j,0}^{-1}\\bar c_j. The first sum combines the cash flows that occur on the same date. The second adds their present values across dates. Paying 100 USD today and receiving 110 USD in one year therefore gives NPV 0 = − 100 + 110 / 1.05 ≈ 4.76 \\operatorname{NPV}_0=-100+110/1.05\\approx4.76 USD. The positive result means that the future payment beats the benchmark requirement by 4.76 USD in today’s money. The discount factor is dimensionless, so each term in the NPV retains units of USD. What if there are two cash flows on the sale date? Receiving 110 USD and paying a 2 USD fee gives c ‾ 1 = 110 ( + 1 ) + 2 ( − 1 ) = 108 \\bar c_1=110(+1)+2(-1)=108 USD. We discount the net amount: − 100 + 108 / 1.05 = 2.86 -100+108/1.05=2.86 USD. This shows the role of each sum: the inner sum nets the cash flows on one date, and the outer sum discounts each date’s net amount. Try it. Keep the 110 USD receipt, with no fee, but raise the benchmark to 10%. What is the NPV? Show the answer NPV becomes − 100 + 110 / 1.10 = 0 -100+110/1.10=0 . The cash gain is still 10 USD, but it now only matches the benchmark. When future receipts are uncertain, this calculation values only the cash-flow scenario we supply. Discounting expresses cash flows from different dates as values on one common date, so we can compare and add them. A positive NPV means that, at the stated benchmark rate, the present value of the future net cash flows exceeds the initial cost.", "url": "questions/discounting.html"}, {"id": "bond-payments", "title": "How do coupon payments and yield determine a bond price?", "theme": "cashflows", "keywords": "Treasury bond bill coupon yield par price discount security", "text": "Where does the investment gain come from? A bill held to maturity makes a single payment, par, and in the example below we buy it for less than par. A coupon bond also pays cash along the way. In both cases, we first identify the dated payments, then use the stated rate convention to connect them to today’s price. From a bill quote to investment growth. The bank-discount quote expresses the discount as a fraction of par. We first calculate the purchase price, then use the ratio of par to the purchase price to calculate the growth rate over the holding period: V B = V P ( 1 − d D 360 ) , g B = 1 T ln ( V P V B ) . V_B=V_P\\left(1-d\\frac{D}{360}\\right),\\qquad g_B=\\frac{1}{T}\\ln\\left(\\frac{V_P}{V_B}\\right). For V P = 100 V_P=100 USD, d = 0.04 d=0.04 , and D = 90 D=90 days, the bill costs 99 USD. If we measure the holding period as T = 90 / 365 T=90/365 years, g B ≈ 0.04076 y r − 1 g_B\\approx0.04076\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The quote is based on par and a 360-day year; the growth calculation is based on the amount invested and the holding-time convention we chose. What changes for a coupon bond? We receive coupons along the way as well as par at maturity. Just after a coupon payment, and with a constant discount yield, the bond’s value is the sum of the remaining discounted payments: V B = V P 𝒟 N , 0 − 1 ⏟ present value of par + C ∑ j = 1 N 𝒟 j , 0 − 1 ⏟ present value of coupons . V_B=\\underbrace{V_P\\mathcal D_{N,0}^{-1}}_{\\text{present value of par}} +\\underbrace{C\\sum_{j=1}^{N}\\mathcal D_{j,0}^{-1}}_{\\text{present value of coupons}}. Each discount factor applies to the date of its payment. This is the L1b cash-flow calculation applied to the bond’s payment schedule. Price a short coupon schedule. Let par be 100 USD and the annual coupon rate be 4%, paid twice per year. Each coupon is C = ( 0.04 / 2 ) ( 100 ) = 2 C=(0.04/2)(100)=2 USD. With one year remaining, the payments are 2 USD in six months and 102 USD in one year. At a 6% nominal yield compounded semiannually: V B = 2 1.03 + 102 1.03 2 = 98.09 U S D . V_B=\\frac{2}{1.03}+\\frac{102}{1.03^2}=98.09\\ \\mathrm{USD}. The coupon rate fixes the promised 2 USD payments, and the yield discounts them. Here the coupon rate is below the yield, so the price is below par. Try it. Keep the same payments but use a 4% yield. What is the price? Show the answer 2 / 1.02 + 102 / 1.02 2 = 100 2/1.02+102/1.02^2=100 USD. The payments are unchanged, but their present values are larger. The coupon rate sets the size of the promised coupon payments. The yield discounts those payments and par to the present. The payment schedule and the yield therefore play different roles.", "url": "questions/bond-payments.html"}, {"id": "duration", "title": "Why does a higher yield lower a bond’s price?", "theme": "cashflows", "keywords": "bond duration convexity yield curve spot par coupon sensitivity", "text": "Why does a higher yield lower a bond’s price? The promised payments are fixed. Raising the yield increases the denominator that discounts each payment, so every present value falls. For example, the two payments from L2a, 2 USD in six months and 102 USD in one year, are worth 100 USD at a 4% nominal yield and 98.09 USD at 6%. An investor who requires more growth from the same cash payments must pay a lower price to get it. What does duration measure? At the 4% yield, the present values of the two payments are 2 / 1.02 = 1.9608 2/1.02=1.9608 and 102 / 1.02 2 = 98.0392 102/1.02^2=98.0392 USD. Dividing each by the 100 USD price gives its weight. Macaulay duration averages the payment times using these weights: D m a c = 0.5 ( 0.019608 ) + 1 ( 0.980392 ) = 0.9902 y r . D_{\\mathrm{mac}}=0.5(0.019608)+1(0.980392)=0.9902\\ \\mathrm{yr}. Most of the value arrives on the last date, so the duration is close to one year. Shifting more present value to the earlier coupon would lower this weighted average time. How much will a yield change move the price? Modified duration gives the first-order price sensitivity; convexity corrects for curvature. With the lecture’s nominal-yield convention: D m o d = D m a c 1 + y / n , Δ V B V B ≈ − D m o d Δ y + 1 2 K ( Δ y ) 2 . D_{\\mathrm{mod}}=\\frac{D_{\\mathrm{mac}}}{1+y/n},\\qquad \\frac{\\Delta V_B}{V_B}\\approx-D_{\\mathrm{mod}}\\Delta y+\\frac12K(\\Delta y)^2. For a different bond with D m o d = 5 D_{\\mathrm{mod}}=5 yr, K = 30 y r 2 K=30\\,\\mathrm{yr}^2 , and a yield increase Δ y = 0.01 y r − 1 \\Delta y=0.01\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , the estimated relative price change is − 0.05 + 0.0015 = − 0.0485 -0.05+0.0015=-0.0485 , or a 4.85% decrease. This local approximation is most useful for small yield changes. How do we value payments with a yield curve? The curve supplies a discount factor D ( 0 , t j ) D(0,t_j) for each payment date. Adding the discounted payments gives the price; setting that price equal to par and solving for the coupon rate gives: V B = ∑ j = 1 N X j D ( 0 , t j ) , c p a r = n 1 − D ( 0 , t N ) ∑ j = 1 N D ( 0 , t j ) . V_B=\\sum_{j=1}^{N}X_jD(0,t_j),\\qquad c_{\\mathrm{par}}=n\\frac{1-D(0,t_N)}{\\sum_{j=1}^{N}D(0,t_j)}. The par coupon makes the present value of the coupons equal to the difference between par and the present value of the final par repayment. For example, if the six-month and one-year discount factors are 0.98 and 0.95, the same two payments are worth 2 ( 0.98 ) + 102 ( 0.95 ) = 98.86 2(0.98)+102(0.95)=98.86 USD. A par coupon must make up the 100 − 100 ( 0.95 ) = 5 100-100(0.95)=5 USD gap between par and the present value of the par repayment, so each coupon is 5 / ( 0.98 + 0.95 ) = 2.5907 5/(0.98+0.95)=2.5907 USD, corresponding to c p a r = 2 ( 2.5907 ) / 100 = 0.05181 y r − 1 c_{\\mathrm{par}}=2(2.5907)/100=0.05181\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Try it. In the duration-convexity example, lower the yield by 0.01 instead. What changes? Show the answer the estimated change is + 0.05 + 0.0015 = + 5.15 % +0.05+0.0015=+5.15\\% . The duration term changes sign; the positive curvature correction does not. With the promised payments fixed, a higher yield discounts every payment more heavily, so the price falls. Duration and convexity summarize how sensitive the price is to small yield changes.", "url": "questions/duration.html"}, {"id": "risk-neutral-probability", "title": "Why do we price options with q instead of our own probability?", "theme": "options", "keywords": "risk neutral probability q real world p beliefs premium expected NPV pricing rule drift", "text": "What does our own probability give us? Suppose a share trades at S 0 = 100 S_{0}=100 USD/share and, one year from now, will be worth either u S 0 = 120 uS_{0}=120 or d S 0 = 90 dS_{0}=90 USD/share, so u = 1.2 u=1.2 and d = 0.9 d=0.9 . A call with strike K = 100 K=100 USD/share pays 20 USD/share after an up move and nothing after a down move. With the risk-free rate g y = 0.04 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.04\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , the one-year discount factor is 𝒟 1 , 0 − 1 ( g y ) = e − 0.04 = 0.9608 \\mathcal{D}^{-1}_{1,0}(g_{y})=e^{-0.04}=0.9608 . As in L9a, a buyer whose real-world up probability is p p has the expected NPV: 𝔼 [ NPV 0 ] = − 𝒫 c + 𝒟 1 , 0 − 1 ( g y ) ( 20 ) p . \\mathbb{E}\\left[\\operatorname{NPV}_{0}\\right]=-\\mathcal{P}_{c}+\\mathcal{D}^{-1}_{1,0}(g_{y})\\,(20)\\,p. A buyer with p = 0.7 p=0.7 has a non-negative expected NPV for any premium up to 0.9608 ( 20 ) ( 0.7 ) = 13.45 0.9608(20)(0.7)=13.45 USD/share. A buyer with p = 0.4 p=0.4 stops at 7.69 USD/share. Each number is the most that buyer would pay, and every belief gives a different one. Which probability gives one market price? The market quotes one premium that every buyer pays, so it prices with one shared rule, the risk-neutral pricing rule ℚ \\mathbb{Q} . That rule must also agree with the prices the market already quotes for the share and for risk-free lending. For a share with no dividends, this means that under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} the expected share price grows at the risk-free rate. On the tree, that condition fixes the up probability: q u S 0 + ( 1 − q ) d S 0 = S 0 e g y Δ t ⇒ q = e g y Δ t − d u − d = 1.04081 − 0.9 1.2 − 0.9 = 0.4694 . q\\,uS_{0}+(1-q)\\,dS_{0}=S_{0}\\,e^{g_{y}\\Delta t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q=\\frac{e^{g_{y}\\Delta t}-d}{u-d}=\\frac{1.04081-0.9}{1.2-0.9}=0.4694. Setting the expected NPV under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} to zero gives the premium: 𝒫 c = 𝒟 1 , 0 − 1 ( g y ) 𝔼 ℚ [ V c ] = 0.9608 ( 20 ) ( 0.4694 ) = 9.02 U S D / s h a r e . \\mathcal{P}_{c}=\\mathcal{D}^{-1}_{1,0}(g_{y})\\,\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbb{Q}}\\left[V_{c}\\right]=0.9608\\,(20)(0.4694)=9.02\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}. Why should the share grow at the risk-free rate? Apply the rule to the share itself. Buying the share for 100 USD and selling it after one year has the expected NPV − 100 + 𝒟 1 , 0 − 1 ( g y ) 𝔼 ℚ [ S 1 ] -100+\\mathcal{D}^{-1}_{1,0}(g_{y})\\,\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbb{Q}}[S_{1}] under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} . This is zero only when 𝔼 ℚ [ S 1 ] = 100 e g y Δ t \\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbb{Q}}[S_{1}]=100\\,e^{g_{y}\\Delta t} , which is the condition we used to find q q . So q q is the one up probability that gives the share’s market price an expected NPV of zero, and the option premium uses the same probability. Under a buyer’s own beliefs, the share’s expected NPV is usually not zero. With p = 0.7 p=0.7 , it is − 100 + 0.9608 ( 0.7 ⋅ 120 + 0.3 ⋅ 90 ) = 6.65 -100+0.9608\\,(0.7\\cdot120+0.3\\cdot90)=6.65 USD/share, because this buyer expects the share to grow faster than the risk-free rate. Why doesn’t the real chance of an up move change the premium? The drift enters only through p p . In the CRR model, u u and d d come from the volatility and the time step (L9b), and q q comes from u u , d d , g y g_{y} , and Δ t \\Delta t . A buyer’s forecast does not move the premium, but it decides whether the premium is a good deal for that buyer. With p = 0.7 p=0.7 , paying 9.02 USD/share gives an expected NPV of 13.45 − 9.02 = 4.43 13.45-9.02=4.43 USD/share under that buyer’s beliefs. The value q = 0.4694 q=0.4694 is not a forecast that 47% of moves will be up. When we forecast how likely a trade is to make money, as with the probability of profit in L10a, we return to our own beliefs ℙ \\mathbb{P} . Try it. Change p p from 0.7 to 0.5. What changes, and what stays the same? Show the answer The most this buyer would pay falls to 0.9608 ( 20 ) ( 0.5 ) = 9.61 0.9608(20)(0.5)=9.61 USD/share, and the share’s expected NPV under this buyer’s beliefs falls to 0.88 USD/share. The market premium stays at 9.02 USD/share, because q q depends only on u u , d d , g y g_{y} , and Δ t \\Delta t . Our own probability tells us the most we would pay, and buyers with different beliefs get different numbers. The market quotes one premium, so it prices with one shared rule, under which the expected share price grows at the risk-free rate.", "url": "questions/risk-neutral-probability.html"}, {"id": "continuation-value", "title": "How do we compute the continuation value at a lattice node?", "theme": "options", "keywords": "continuation value hold exercise backward induction binomial lattice American put Hull extrinsic early exercise premium node", "text": "What are the two values at a node? At node ( t , k ) (t,k) of the lattice, with share price S t , k S_{t,k} , the American holder compares exercising now, which pays V ( K , S t , k ) V(K,S_{t,k}) , with holding for one more step. The value of holding is the continuation value: H t , k = 𝒟 t + 1 , t − 1 ( g y ) ⋅ ( q V ̂ t + 1 , k + 1 + ( 1 − q ) V ̂ t + 1 , k ) . H_{t,k}=\\mathcal{D}^{-1}_{t+1,t}(g_{y})\\cdot\\bigl(q\\,\\hat{V}_{t+1,k+1}+(1-q)\\,\\hat{V}_{t+1,k}\\bigr). The node value is the larger of the two: V ̂ t , k = max { V ( K , S t , k ) , H t , k } . \\hat{V}_{t,k}=\\max\\bigl\\{V(K,S_{t,k}),~H_{t,k}\\bigr\\}. We weight the values at the up and down nodes by q q and 1 − q 1-q , add them, and discount one step with 𝒟 t + 1 , t − 1 ( g y ) = e − g y Δ t \\mathcal{D}^{-1}_{t+1,t}(g_{y})=e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t} . The values at the next nodes must already be known, so we start at expiration, where each node value is the payoff, and work backward to the root. Hull’s two-step put, node by node. The L9b example has S 0 = 50 S_{0}=50 USD/share, K = 52 K=52 USD/share, u = 1.2 u=1.2 , d = 0.8 d=0.8 , Δ t = 1 \\Delta t=1 year, and g y = 0.05 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.05\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , so q = 0.6282 q=0.6282 , 1 − q = 0.3718 1-q=0.3718 , and e − 0.05 = 0.9512 e^{-0.05}=0.9512 . At expiration the share prices are 72, 48, and 32 USD/share, and the put pays 0, 4, and 20 USD/share. Working backward: Node Exercise Continuation value Value Up, S 1 , 1 = 60 S_{1,1}=60 0 0.9512 ( 0.6282 ⋅ 0 + 0.3718 ⋅ 4 ) = 1.41 0.9512\\,(0.6282\\cdot0+0.3718\\cdot4)=1.41 1.41 Down, S 1 , 0 = 40 S_{1,0}=40 12 0.9512 ( 0.6282 ⋅ 4 + 0.3718 ⋅ 20 ) = 9.46 0.9512\\,(0.6282\\cdot4+0.3718\\cdot20)=9.46 12 Root, S 0 = 50 S_{0}=50 2 0.9512 ( 0.6282 ⋅ 1.41 + 0.3718 ⋅ 12 ) = 5.09 0.9512\\,(0.6282\\cdot1.41+0.3718\\cdot12)=5.09 5.09 The holder holds at the up node and at the root, and exercises at the down node. At the root, the down branch uses the node value 12, not the continuation value 9.46: each earlier node uses the values after the decision at the later nodes. That is how one early exercise at t = 1 t=1 raises the root value. The European put must hold at the down node, so its root value is 0.9512 ( 0.6282 ⋅ 1.41 + 0.3718 ⋅ 9.46 ) = 4.19 0.9512\\,(0.6282\\cdot1.41+0.3718\\cdot9.46)=4.19 USD/share. How do extrinsic value and the early-exercise premium differ? Both are differences, but they compare different things. Extrinsic value compares the American contract’s value at a node with exercising now at that node, E V = V ̂ t , k − V ( K , S t , k ) E_{V}=\\hat{V}_{t,k}-V(K,S_{t,k}) . At the root, E V = 5.09 − 2 = 3.09 E_{V}=5.09-2=3.09 USD/share. At the down node, E V = 12 − 12 = 0 E_{V}=12-12=0 , because the holder exercises there. The early-exercise premium compares two contracts at the root, 𝒫 AM − 𝒫 EU = 5.09 − 4.19 = 0.90 \\mathcal{P}^{\\text{AM}}-\\mathcal{P}^{\\text{EU}}=5.09-4.19=0.90 USD/share. A contract can have a large extrinsic value and no early-exercise premium: an at-the-money call on a share with no dividends is an example (L10a). Try it. What is the extrinsic value at the up node at t = 1 t=1 ? Show the answer Exercising pays nothing there, because the share price of 60 USD/share is above the strike, and the node value is 1.41 USD/share. The whole value, E V = 1.41 − 0 = 1.41 E_{V}=1.41-0=1.41 USD/share, is extrinsic. Weight the option values at the two next nodes by the risk-neutral probabilities, add them, and discount one step. The American node value is the larger of this continuation value and the payoff from exercising now.", "url": "questions/continuation-value.html"}, {"id": "early-exercise", "title": "Why can early exercise pay for a put but not for a call?", "theme": "options", "keywords": "early exercise American put call dividends interest strike optimal exercise European index options", "text": "What does a put holder gain by exercising early? Take the down node at t = 1 t=1 in Hull’s two-step put (L9b): S 1 , 0 = 40 S_{1,0}=40 USD/share, K = 52 K=52 USD/share, one step of Δ t = 1 \\Delta t=1 year remains, and g y = 0.05 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.05\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Both possible share prices at expiration, 48 and 32 USD/share, are below the strike, so the put pays K − S 2 K-S_{2} either way. The share pays no dividends, so under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} the expected share price grows at the risk-free rate, 𝔼 ℚ [ S 2 ] = S 1 , 0 e g y Δ t \\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbb{Q}}[S_{2}]=S_{1,0}\\,e^{g_{y}\\Delta t} , so the continuation value is: H 1 , 0 = e − g y Δ t 𝔼 ℚ [ K − S 2 ] = K e − g y Δ t − S 1 , 0 = 52 ( 0.9512 ) − 40 = 9.46 U S D / s h a r e . H_{1,0}=e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t}\\,\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbb{Q}}\\left[K-S_{2}\\right]=K\\,e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t}-S_{1,0}=52(0.9512)-40=9.46\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}. Exercising now pays K − S 1 , 0 = 12 K-S_{1,0}=12 USD/share. The difference, K ( 1 − e − g y Δ t ) = 52 ( 1 − 0.9512 ) = 2.54 K\\bigl(1-e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t}\\bigr)=52(1-0.9512)=2.54 USD/share, is the interest on the strike price: exercising collects the 52 USD/share one year sooner. Here waiting adds no value of its own: the put finishes in the money in both outcomes, so the choice not to sell at K K is never used. What does the holder give up in general? The put payoff splits as ( K − S ) + = ( K − S ) + ( S − K ) + (K-S)^{+}=(K-S)+(S-K)^{+} , where x + = max ( x , 0 ) x^{+}=\\max(x,0) . At a node one step before expiration, this gives: H t , k = K e − g y Δ t − S t , k ⏟ sell at K one step later + e − g y Δ t 𝔼 ℚ [ ( S t + 1 − K ) + ] ⏟ value of not selling if S t + 1 > K . H_{t,k}=\\underbrace{K\\,e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t}-S_{t,k}}_{\\text{sell at }K\\text{ one step later}}+\\underbrace{e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t}\\,\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbb{Q}}\\left[(S_{t+1}-K)^{+}\\right]}_{\\text{value of not selling if }S_{t+1}>K}. The second term is what holding is worth when the share price rises above the strike: the holder does not have to sell at K K . Exercising beats holding when the interest on the strike, K ( 1 − e − g y Δ t ) K\\bigl(1-e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t}\\bigr) , is larger than that second term. Deep in the money, the share price is unlikely to rise above K K , so the second term is small, and with a positive risk-free rate, early exercise pays. Closer to the strike, the second term is larger and holding wins. Being in the money is not enough by itself. Why is a call different? Exercising a call pays ( S t , k − K ) + (S_{t,k}-K)^{+} . The value at each next node is at least that node’s payoff, which is at least both S t + 1 − K S_{t+1}-K and zero, so the continuation value is at least zero and: H t , k ≥ e − g y Δ t 𝔼 ℚ [ S t + 1 − K ] = S t , k − K e − g y Δ t ≥ S t , k − K when g y ≥ 0 . H_{t,k}\\geq e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t}\\,\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbb{Q}}\\left[S_{t+1}-K\\right]=S_{t,k}-K\\,e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t}\\geq S_{t,k}-K\\quad\\text{when }g_{y}\\geq0. Together, H t , k ≥ ( S t , k − K ) + H_{t,k}\\geq(S_{t,k}-K)^{+} . Exercising a call pays the strike sooner, which loses interest, and it gives up the choice not to buy if the share price falls below K K . For a share with no dividends and a non-negative risk-free rate, exercising early is never strictly better, so the American call has the same premium as the European call (L10a). A dividend changes this: the holder must own the shares before the ex-dividend date to collect it, so exercising just before that date can pay (L10a). Why do European contracts exist? For the buyer, the American right is worth at least as much as the European one (L9a). European-style contracts are the convention for cash-settled index options such as SPX (L9a). The seller of a European contract cannot be assigned before expiration. Try it. At Hull’s down node, set g y = 0 g_{y}=0 . Would the American holder still exercise? Show the answer No longer strictly. With g y = 0 g_{y}=0 , the interest on the strike is zero and H 1 , 0 = 52 − 40 = 12 H_{1,0}=52-40=12 USD/share, the same as exercising. The holder is indifferent, and this node adds nothing to the early-exercise premium. Exercising a put early collects the strike price sooner, and the interest on it can outweigh what we give up by not waiting. Exercising a call early pays the strike price sooner, so without dividends and with a non-negative rate, holding is never worse.", "url": "questions/early-exercise.html"}, {"id": "crr-factors", "title": "Where do the CRR up and down factors come from?", "theme": "options", "keywords": "CRR up down factors u d volatility moment matching binomial lattice time step Cox Ross Rubinstein", "text": "What must one step of the tree reproduce? The CRR model makes a down move undo an up move, u d = 1 ud=1 . With a = ln u a=\\ln u , the growth rate over one step of length Δ t \\Delta t is + a / Δ t +a/\\Delta t with the real-world probability p p and − a / Δ t -a/\\Delta t with probability 1 − p 1-p . Under geometric Brownian motion, the growth rate over Δ t \\Delta t has mean μ g \\mu_{g} and variance σ 2 / Δ t \\sigma^{2}/\\Delta t . Matching the two moments gives (L9b): ( 2 p − 1 ) a Δ t = μ g , 4 p ( 1 − p ) a 2 Δ t 2 = σ 2 Δ t ⇒ a 2 = σ 2 Δ t + μ g 2 Δ t 2 . \\frac{(2p-1)\\,a}{\\Delta t}=\\mu_{g}, \\qquad \\frac{4p(1-p)\\,a^{2}}{\\Delta t^{2}}=\\frac{\\sigma^{2}}{\\Delta t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a^{2}=\\sigma^{2}\\Delta t+\\mu_{g}^{2}\\Delta t^{2}. When can we drop the second term? The second term equals the first times ( μ g / σ ) 2 Δ t (\\mu_{g}/\\sigma)^{2}\\Delta t . Take σ = 0.30 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.30\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} and μ g = 0.10 y r − 1 \\mu_{g}=0.10\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . With daily steps, Δ t = 1 / 365 \\Delta t=1/365 year, the ratio is 0.0003, and keeping the term would change a a by about 0.015%. With a single one-year step, the ratio is 0.11, and a a would change by 5.4%. Lattice pricing uses many small steps, so Cox, Ross, and Rubinstein keep only the first term, a = σ Δ t a=\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t} : u = e σ Δ t , d = e − σ Δ t . u=e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}},\\qquad d=e^{-\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}. For the daily step, u = 1.01583 u=1.01583 and d = 0.98442 d=0.98442 . Why match real-world moments when we price with q? After the second term is dropped, a a depends on neither μ g \\mu_{g} nor p p : the size of a move is set by the volatility alone, and the volatility is the same under ℙ \\mathbb{P} and ℚ \\mathbb{Q} (L9b). One tree therefore serves both questions, and only the up probability changes. For forecasting, the mean condition gives p = 1 2 ( 1 + ( μ g / σ ) Δ t ) = 0.5087 p=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\bigl(1+(\\mu_{g}/\\sigma)\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\bigr)=0.5087 for the daily step. For pricing, q = ( e g y Δ t − d ) / ( u − d ) q=(e^{g_{y}\\Delta t}-d)/(u-d) , which is 0.4996 with g y = 0.04 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.04\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . For a small step, q ≈ 1 2 + g y − σ 2 / 2 2 σ Δ t q\\approx\\tfrac{1}{2}+\\frac{g_{y}-\\sigma^{2}/2}{2\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\Delta t} , the formula for p p with g y − σ 2 / 2 g_{y}-\\sigma^{2}/2 in place of μ g \\mu_{g} . The advanced notebook shows that with q q the tree’s mean and variance approach those of geometric Brownian motion under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} as the step shrinks. Does q stay between zero and one? We need d < e g y Δ t < u d<e^{g_{y}\\Delta t}<u , which for the CRR factors becomes | g y | Δ t < σ \\lvert g_{y}\\rvert\\sqrt{\\Delta t}<\\sigma . With daily steps and g y = 0.04 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.04\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , the volatility must exceed 0.04 1 / 365 = 0.0021 y r − 1 / 2 0.04\\sqrt{1/365}=0.0021\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , far below the volatilities in an options chain. The L9b pricing example checks this floor for every contract. Try it. Halve the volatility to σ = 0.15 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.15\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , with daily steps and the same μ g \\mu_{g} . What happens to u u and to the dropped term? Show the answer u = e 0.15 / 365 = 1.00788 u=e^{0.15/\\sqrt{365}}=1.00788 . The ratio of the dropped term to the kept term becomes ( 0.10 / 0.15 ) 2 / 365 = 0.0012 (0.10/0.15)^{2}/365=0.0012 , still small, so dropping it changes a a by about 0.06%. We match the mean and variance of one lattice step to geometric Brownian motion. For a small time step, the size of a move depends only on the volatility, so the same up and down factors serve forecasting and pricing.", "url": "questions/crr-factors.html"}, {"id": "bsm-formula", "title": "What does each term in the BSM call formula mean?", "theme": "options", "keywords": "Black Scholes Merton BSM formula d+ d- N(d) normal CDF probability of exercise call put premium", "text": "What does the formula compute? The Black–Scholes–Merton (BSM) formula gives the same premium that the Monte Carlo simulation estimates in L9a: the discounted expected payoff under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} of a European call on a share with no dividends. When the call is exercised, the holder receives a share worth S ( T ) S(T) and pays K K . Let 𝟙 { S ( T ) > K } \\mathbb{1}\\{S(T)>K\\} equal 1 when the call finishes in the money and 0 otherwise. Splitting the payoff into the two cash flows gives: 𝒫 c = 𝒟 T , 0 − 1 ( g y ) 𝔼 ℚ [ S ( T ) 𝟙 { S ( T ) > K } ] ⏟ S ( 0 ) N ( d + ) : value today of the share received − K 𝒟 T , 0 − 1 ( g y ) ℚ ( S ( T ) > K ) ⏟ K 𝒟 T , 0 − 1 ( g y ) N ( d − ) : value today of the strike paid . \\mathcal{P}_{c}=\\underbrace{\\mathcal{D}^{-1}_{T,0}(g_{y})\\,\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbb{Q}}\\left[S(T)\\,\\mathbb{1}\\{S(T)>K\\}\\right]}_{S(0)\\,N(d_{+})\\text{: value today of the share received}}-\\underbrace{K\\,\\mathcal{D}^{-1}_{T,0}(g_{y})\\,\\mathbb{Q}\\bigl(S(T)>K\\bigr)}_{K\\,\\mathcal{D}^{-1}_{T,0}(g_{y})\\,N(d_{-})\\text{: value today of the strike paid}}. Where does N ( d − ) N(d_{-}) come from? Under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} , geometric Brownian motion with drift g y g_{y} gives ln S ( T ) = ln S ( 0 ) + ( g y − σ 2 2 ) T + σ T Z \\ln S(T)=\\ln S(0)+\\bigl(g_{y}-\\tfrac{\\sigma^{2}}{2}\\bigr)T+\\sigma\\sqrt{T}\\,Z , where Z Z is a standard normal random variable. The call finishes in the money when: S ( T ) > K ⇔ Z > − ln ( S ( 0 ) / K ) + ( g y − σ 2 2 ) T σ T = − d − . S(T)>K \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad Z>-\\frac{\\ln\\bigl(S(0)/K\\bigr)+\\bigl(g_{y}-\\tfrac{\\sigma^{2}}{2}\\bigr)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt{T}}=-d_{-}. The normal distribution is symmetric, so ℚ ( Z > − d − ) = N ( d − ) \\mathbb{Q}(Z>-d_{-})=N(d_{-}) , the risk-neutral probability that the call finishes in the money. The first term uses the same event but weights each exercised outcome by its share price, so N ( d + ) N(d_{+}) is not a probability of exercise. Higher share prices get more weight, so N ( d + ) > N ( d − ) N(d_{+})>N(d_{-}) . The contingent claims notebook builds the call premium from these two pieces. A worked call. Take S ( 0 ) = 100 S(0)=100 USD/share, K = 105 K=105 USD/share, T = 0.25 T=0.25 year, σ = 0.30 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.30\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , and g y = 0.04 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.04\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Then d + = − 0.1836 d_{+}=-0.1836 , d − = d + − σ T = − 0.3336 d_{-}=d_{+}-\\sigma\\sqrt{T}=-0.3336 , N ( d + ) = 0.42716 N(d_{+})=0.42716 , and N ( d − ) = 0.36934 N(d_{-})=0.36934 . The discounted strike is 105 e − 0.01 = 103.955 105\\,e^{-0.01}=103.955 USD/share, so: 𝒫 c = 100 ( 0.42716 ) − 103.955 ( 0.36934 ) = 42.716 − 38.395 = 4.32 U S D / s h a r e . \\mathcal{P}_{c}=100(0.42716)-103.955(0.36934)=42.716-38.395=4.32\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}. Under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} , this call finishes in the money with probability 0.37. The BSM put formula gives 𝒫 p = 8.28 \\mathcal{P}_{p}=8.28 USD/share, and put–call parity checks both: 4.32 − 8.28 = − 3.96 = 100 − 103.96 4.32-8.28=-3.96=100-103.96 . How do the BSM equation and the lattice fit in? The BSM equation is a partial differential equation for the premium. We do not solve it in this course; L9a states that its solution, worked backward from the payoff, equals the risk-neutral premium, and the contingent claims notebook derives it. A European premium computed on a CRR lattice, where the holder always holds, approaches the BSM premium as the time step shrinks: with 91 steps, about one per day, the lattice gives 4.31 USD/share for this call. The BSM formula has no early exercise, so an American contract whose early exercise can pay, such as a put, needs a method that models it: the CRR lattice (L9b) or Longstaff–Schwartz simulation (L10a). Try it. Lower the strike to K = 100 K=100 USD/share with everything else unchanged. What is the risk-neutral probability that the call finishes in the money? Show the answer d − = ( 0 + ( 0.04 − 0.045 ) ( 0.25 ) ) / 0.15 = − 0.0083 d_{-}=\\bigl(0+(0.04-0.045)(0.25)\\bigr)/0.15=-0.0083 , so N ( d − ) = 0.4967 N(d_{-})=0.4967 . A lower strike makes exercise more likely, and the premium rises to 6.46 USD/share. The BSM call premium is the discounted expected payoff under the risk-neutral pricing rule. One term values the share received at exercise, the other values the strike paid, and N of d-minus is the risk-neutral probability that the call finishes in the money.", "url": "questions/bsm-formula.html"}, {"id": "implied-volatility", "title": "Which volatility goes into the premium, and how do we find implied volatility?", "theme": "options", "keywords": "implied volatility historical volatility bisection IV smile skew one standard deviation range sigma", "text": "Why does volatility matter to the premium? A call pays ( S ( T ) − K ) + (S(T)-K)^{+} , and a put pays ( K − S ( T ) ) + (K-S(T))^{+} . Neither payoff falls below zero. In the BSM model, with the other inputs fixed, a larger volatility spreads out the share price at expiration, which adds outcomes with large payoffs and costs the holder nothing in the outcomes where the payoff is already zero. So the premium rises with σ \\sigma . Which volatility? L9a gives two ways to put a number on σ \\sigma : Historical volatility Implied volatility Source Past share prices: σ a n n = Δ t ⋅ σ g \\sigma_{\\mathrm{ann}}=\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\cdot\\sigma_{g} The market premium of an option Direction Looks backward Looks forward Use Our forecast under ℙ \\mathbb{P} Comparing a model premium with market quotes Here σ g \\sigma_{g} is the standard deviation of the growth rates (units: y r − 1 \\mathrm{yr}^{-1} ) and Δ t \\Delta t is the time between observations (units: years). Trading platforms compute the Greeks with the implied volatility (L10a). How does bisection find the implied volatility? We look for the σ \\sigma whose BSM premium equals the market premium. Take a European call with S ( 0 ) = 100 S(0)=100 USD/share, K = 105 K=105 USD/share, T = 0.25 T=0.25 year, and g y = 0.04 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.04\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , quoted at 6.00 USD/share. The bracket σ lo = 0.05 \\sigma_{\\text{lo}}=0.05 and σ hi = 1.00 \\sigma_{\\text{hi}}=1.00 gives BSM premiums of 0.07 and 18.21 USD/share, which bracket 6.00. Each step prices the midpoint. A premium above 6.00 USD/share moves the upper end down, and one below moves the lower end up: Step σ lo \\sigma_{\\text{lo}} σ hi \\sigma_{\\text{hi}} σ mid \\sigma_{\\text{mid}} 𝒫 ( σ mid ) \\mathcal{P}(\\sigma_{\\text{mid}}) Update 1 0.0500 1.0000 0.5250 8.79 σ hi ← σ mid \\sigma_{\\text{hi}}\\gets\\sigma_{\\text{mid}} 2 0.0500 0.5250 0.2875 4.08 σ lo ← σ mid \\sigma_{\\text{lo}}\\gets\\sigma_{\\text{mid}} 3 0.2875 0.5250 0.4063 6.42 σ hi ← σ mid \\sigma_{\\text{hi}}\\gets\\sigma_{\\text{mid}} 4 0.2875 0.4063 0.3469 5.24 σ lo ← σ mid \\sigma_{\\text{lo}}\\gets\\sigma_{\\text{mid}} 5 0.3469 0.4063 0.3766 5.83 σ lo ← σ mid \\sigma_{\\text{lo}}\\gets\\sigma_{\\text{mid}} Each step halves the bracket. After 14 steps, the bracket is narrower than 10 − 4 10^{-4} , and the implied volatility is σ ̂ = 0.385 y r − 1 / 2 \\hat{\\sigma}=0.385\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} . What does the implied volatility tell us? L9a uses it for a back-of-the-envelope one-standard-deviation range for the share price, S ( 0 ) ( 1 ± IV T ) = 100 ( 1 ± 0.385 ⋅ 0.5 ) S(0)\\bigl(1\\pm\\text{IV}\\sqrt{T}\\bigr)=100\\,(1\\pm0.385\\cdot0.5) , or about 80.75 to 119.25 USD/share over the next three months. Under the model, the share price ends inside this range about 68% of the time. Why does each strike have its own implied volatility? The BSM model assumes one constant σ \\sigma for every strike and expiration. Backing out σ \\sigma strike by strike from market quotes gives a different value at each strike, the volatility smile or skew. The market’s premiums are not consistent with a single constant volatility. The SPXW volatility skew notebook measures this skew for S&P 500 index options. Try it. If the same call were quoted at 4.32 USD/share, what implied volatility would bisection return? Show the answer About 0.30, because 4.32 USD/share is the BSM premium of this call at σ = 0.30 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.30\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} . Historical volatility is estimated from past share prices. Implied volatility is the value that makes the BSM premium of a European contract equal its market premium, and bisection finds it because the premium rises with volatility.", "url": "questions/implied-volatility.html"}, {"id": "delta-poe-gap", "title": "Why is a call's Delta close to, but larger than, its probability of exercise?", "theme": "options", "keywords": "Delta probability of exercise POE q star q* N(d+) N(d-) risk-neutral CRR tree hedge ratio sigma sqrt T gap derivative premium", "text": "Where does the extra factor come from? On a CRR tree with h h steps, the premium of a call on a share with no dividends is a discounted, probability-weighted sum over the terminal nodes (L10a). Delta is the slope of the premium with respect to today’s share price S 0 S_{0} . The tree’s u u , d d , and q q do not depend on S 0 S_{0} , so only the payoffs move. An in-the-money payoff S 0 u k d h − k − K S_{0}u^{k}d^{h-k}-K rises by u k d h − k u^{k}d^{h-k} for each USD/share added to S 0 S_{0} , and u k d h − k = S T / S 0 u^{k}d^{h-k}=S_{T}/S_{0} is the factor by which the share grew to reach node k k . Out-of-the-money payoffs stay at zero. So: Δ c = 𝒟 T , 0 − 1 ( g y ) ∑ k ∈ ITM ( h k ) q k ( 1 − q ) h − k ⏟ ℚ probability of node k u k d h − k ⏟ S T / S 0 ℚ ( S T > K ) = ∑ k ∈ ITM ( h k ) q k ( 1 − q ) h − k . \\begin{aligned} \\Delta_{c}&=\\mathcal{D}^{-1}_{T,0}(g_{y})\\sum_{k\\,\\in\\,\\text{ITM}}\\underbrace{\\binom{h}{k}q^{k}(1-q)^{h-k}}_{\\mathbb{Q}\\text{ probability of node }k}\\;\\underbrace{u^{k}d^{h-k}}_{S_{T}/S_{0}}\\\\ \\mathbb{Q}\\left(S_{T}>K\\right)&=\\sum_{k\\,\\in\\,\\text{ITM}}\\binom{h}{k}q^{k}(1-q)^{h-k}. \\end{aligned} The probability of exercise (POE) under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} counts each in-the-money node by its probability. Delta also weights it by how much the share grew to get there, discounted to today. To get the lecture’s proposition, split the discount factor e − g y h Δ t e^{-g_{y}h\\Delta t} into one factor e − g y Δ t e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t} per step. Each up step then carries the weight q * = q u e − g y Δ t q^{*}=q\\,u\\,e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t} , and each down step the weight ( 1 − q ) d e − g y Δ t = 1 − q * (1-q)\\,d\\,e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t}=1-q^{*} . The two weights add to one because, under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} , the expected share price grows at the risk-free rate. So Delta is itself a probability of finishing in the money, computed with the up probability q * q^{*} . Why is Delta larger? The tree only has q < 1 q<1 when u > e g y Δ t u>e^{g_{y}\\Delta t} , so q * = q u e − g y Δ t > q q^{*}=q\\,u\\,e^{-g_{y}\\Delta t}>q . A larger up probability moves weight toward the nodes with the most up moves. For a call, those are exactly the in-the-money nodes, so their total weight rises, and Δ c > ℚ ( S T > K ) \\Delta_{c}>\\mathbb{Q}(S_{T}>K) . A two-step example. Take S 0 = 100 S_{0}=100 USD/share, K = 95 K=95 USD/share, T = 0.5 T=0.5 year, h = 2 h=2 steps of Δ t = 0.25 \\Delta t=0.25 year, σ = 0.30 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.30\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , and g y = 0.04 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.04\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Then u = e 0.15 = 1.1618 u=e^{0.15}=1.1618 , d = 0.8607 d=0.8607 , q = 0.4959 q=0.4959 , and q * = q u e − 0.01 = 0.5705 q^{*}=q\\,u\\,e^{-0.01}=0.5705 . The terminal share prices are 134.99, 100.00, and 74.08 USD/share, so the two top nodes are in the money, and each probability is one minus the chance of two down moves: ℚ ( S T > K ) = 1 − ( 1 − q ) 2 = 1 − 0.5041 2 = 0.7459 , Δ c = 1 − ( 1 − q * ) 2 = 1 − 0.4295 2 = 0.8155 . \\begin{aligned} \\mathbb{Q}\\left(S_{T}>K\\right)&=1-(1-q)^{2}=1-0.5041^{2}=0.7459,\\\\ \\Delta_{c}&=1-\\left(1-q^{*}\\right)^{2}=1-0.4295^{2}=0.8155. \\end{aligned} Repricing the tree at S 0 = 100.01 S_{0}=100.01 and 99.99 99.99 USD/share and taking the central difference gives the same Delta, 0.8155. Switching from q q to q * q^{*} raises the top node’s weight from 0.2460 to 0.3254 and lowers the bottom node’s from 0.2541 to 0.1845, so the in-the-money total rises. How far apart can they be? As the tree steps shrink, Delta becomes N ( d + ) N(d_{+}) and the POE under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} becomes N ( d − ) N(d_{-}) , and d + − d − = σ T d_{+}-d_{-}=\\sigma\\sqrt{T} . For an at-the-money call with S 0 = K = 100 S_{0}=K=100 USD/share and g y = 0.04 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.04\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} : Contract σ T \\sigma\\sqrt{T} Delta, N ( d + ) N(d_{+}) POE under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} , N ( d − ) N(d_{-}) Gap 30 days, σ = 0.30 \\sigma=0.30 0.086 0.532 0.498 0.034 1 year, σ = 0.50 \\sigma=0.50 0.500 0.629 0.433 0.197 So Delta is a good estimate of the POE under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} for short contracts on calm shares, and a poor one for long contracts or volatile shares. Both numbers come from the pricing rule ℚ \\mathbb{Q} ; neither is our forecast. Which probability should we use? compares them with a forecast under ℙ \\mathbb{P} . Try it. In the two-step example, raise the strike to K = 101 K=101 USD/share, so only the top node is in the money. What are the POE under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} and Delta? Show the answer ℚ ( S T > K ) = q 2 = 0.2460 \\mathbb{Q}(S_{T}>K)=q^{2}=0.2460 and Δ c = ( q * ) 2 = 0.3254 \\Delta_{c}=(q^{*})^{2}=0.3254 . Delta is still larger, because q * > q q^{*}>q . Both add up the same in-the-money outcomes at expiration. The probability of exercise under Q weights each outcome by its chance of happening. Delta also weights it by how much the share grew to get there, discounted to today, so outcomes with more up moves count for more. That makes a call's Delta a little larger, and the gap grows with the volatility times the square root of the time to expiration.", "url": "questions/delta-poe-gap.html"}, {"id": "which-probability", "title": "When Delta, the risk-neutral POE, and our forecast disagree, which should we use?", "theme": "options", "keywords": "Delta risk-neutral Q real-world P forecast POE POP drift estimate historical simulation which probability trust seller conservative AMD NVDA", "text": "Three numbers for one call. In the L10a example, we sell an NVDA call with strike K = 235 K=235 USD/share, 43 days to expiration, a premium of 9.88 USD/share, and an implied volatility of 0.3543. Our forecast ℙ \\mathbb{P} is geometric Brownian motion with the historical drift μ ̂ = 0.5788 y r − 1 \\hat{\\mu}=0.5788\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} and volatility σ ̂ = 0.3886 y r − 1 / 2 \\hat{\\sigma}=0.3886\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} . The risk-neutral ℚ \\mathbb{Q} uses the drift g y = 0.0413 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.0413\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} and the implied volatility: Delta (quoted) POE under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} POE under ℙ \\mathbb{P} Probability the call is exercised 0.4833 0.4295 0.6185 Seller’s POP 0.6976 0.5034 What does each number answer? Delta is the hedge ratio the trading platform computes under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} . Read as a probability, it sits slightly above the POE under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} ( why Delta is larger ). It is not a worst case for a seller: here our forecast puts the chance of exercise at 0.62, well above Delta. The probabilities under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} are the ones the market price is built on. Under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} , buying or selling at the market price has an expected NPV of about zero ( why we price with q ). So the seller’s POP of 0.70 under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} is not, by itself, a reason to sell. The seller wins a small amount often and loses a large amount now and then, and under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} the two balance. “Risk-neutral” names this pricing rule; it does not say that the trade has no risk. The probabilities under ℙ \\mathbb{P} are our own forecast, and we decide with them. Under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} , a trade at the market price is worth about zero, so a trade can only look good to us when our forecast differs from ℚ \\mathbb{Q} . Be careful about why it differs. ℚ \\mathbb{Q} is a pricing rule, not the market’s forecast. If we expect a share to grow faster than the risk-free rate, bullish trades on it will look good under ℙ \\mathbb{P} . Part of that advantage is just the extra growth we expect for holding a risky share; it does not mean the option is mispriced. Here our forecast says the call is more likely to be exercised than the market implies, so selling it looks worse to us, not better. How much should we trust our forecast? In the L11a example, a put credit spread on AMD has a POP of 0.714 under our forecast and 0.540 under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} . The gap comes mostly from the drift: our estimate of AMD’s mean growth rate is μ ̂ g = 0.44 y r − 1 \\hat{\\mu}_{g}=0.44\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The drift is the hardest parameter to estimate. For geometric Brownian motion, the L4b drift-uncertainty notebook shows that a mean growth rate fitted to T span T_{\\text{span}} years of prices has a standard error of about 6 / 5 σ ̂ / T span \\sqrt{6/5}\\,\\hat{\\sigma}/\\sqrt{T_{\\text{span}}} . With ten years of AMD’s σ ̂ = 0.47 \\hat{\\sigma}=0.47 , that is about 0.16 y r − 1 0.16\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The much narrower bounds printed with the AMD parameters assume that the errors around the fitted line are unrelated to one another, which is not true for prices that follow geometric Brownian motion. Keeping σ ̂ \\hat{\\sigma} fixed and lowering the growth rate shows how much of the advantage depends on it: Mean growth rate μ g \\mu_{g} ( y r − 1 \\mathrm{yr}^{-1} ) 0.44 0.20 0.00 POP under ℙ \\mathbb{P} 0.714 0.646 0.584 Even with no growth, the POP stays above 0.540, mostly because the mean growth rate under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} is g y − σ 2 / 2 = − 0.12 y r − 1 g_{y}-\\sigma^{2}/2=-0.12\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , below zero. The wider implied volatility, 0.56 against our 0.47, accounts for only about 0.01. So most of the gap rests on the drift. A practical reading. Compute the probabilities under both. Where they agree, our model and the market see the trade the same way. Where they disagree, find which input causes the gap, the drift or the volatility, and recompute with a more cautious value. If the trade only looks good with the most favorable drift, it rests on a number we cannot estimate precisely. Try it. Set our forecast’s drift to g y g_{y} and its volatility to the implied volatility. What POP do we get for the AMD spread, and what does that say about the trade? Show the answer The POP under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} , 0.540. Our forecast then matches ℚ \\mathbb{Q} , so the trade has an expected NPV of about zero for us, and we have no reason to prefer it. They answer different questions. Delta and the probabilities under Q describe how the market prices the option. Our forecast under P describes what we think will happen. We decide with our forecast, but it is only as good as our estimate of the drift, the input we know least well.", "url": "questions/which-probability.html"}, {"id": "lsm-regression", "title": "How does the Longstaff–Schwartz regression decide when to exercise early?", "theme": "options", "keywords": "Longstaff Schwartz LSM least squares regression continuation value hold exercise American put Monte Carlo in-the-money paths realized cash flows fitted H hat", "text": "What does the regression estimate? At date t t , the holder of an American put knows today’s share price S i ( t ) S_{i}(t) , but not where the path goes next. The value of holding is the expected discounted cash flow from holding, given today’s share price. A single path’s cash flow Y i ( t ) Y_{i}(t) depends on that path’s own future, which the holder cannot see. Regressing Y i ( t ) Y_{i}(t) on S i ( t ) S_{i}(t) fits one curve through the cash flows of all the in-the-money paths, so each path’s own future is just one point in the fit. The fitted curve H ̂ t ( S ) = β 0 + β 1 S + β 2 S 2 \\hat{H}_{t}(S)=\\beta_{0}+\\beta_{1}S+\\beta_{2}S^{2} estimates the value of holding at each share price. One backward step on the eight paths. In the L10a example, K = 1.10 K=1.10 USD/share, g y = 0.06 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.06\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , and the exercise dates are one year apart. At t = 2 t=2 , five paths are in the money. Discounting their later cash flows one step and fitting gives the paper’s reported fit, H ̂ 2 ( S ) = − 1.070 + 2.983 S − 1.813 S 2 \\hat{H}_{2}(S)=-1.070+2.983S-1.813S^{2} . The table compares exercising now, V ( K , S i ( 2 ) ) V(K,S_{i}(2)) , with the realized cash flow from holding, Y i ( 2 ) Y_{i}(2) , and the fitted value of holding, H ̂ 2 ( S i ( 2 ) ) \\hat{H}_{2}(S_{i}(2)) (USD/share): Path S i ( 2 ) S_{i}(2) V V Y i ( 2 ) Y_{i}(2) H ̂ 2 \\hat{H}_{2} Decision 1 1.08 0.02 0.000 0.037 hold 3 1.07 0.03 0.066 0.046 hold 4 0.97 0.13 0.170 0.118 exercise 6 0.77 0.33 0.188 0.152 exercise 7 0.84 0.26 0.085 0.156 exercise Look at path 4. With hindsight, holding was better: its realized cash flow from holding, 0.170, beats exercising for 0.13. At t = 2 t=2 , though, the rule compares the payoff now with the fitted curve at S 4 ( 2 ) = 0.97 S_{4}(2)=0.97 , which puts the value of holding at 0.118. So the rule exercises. The decision depends on path 4 only through its share price today. Why fit only the in-the-money paths? An out-of-the-money path pays nothing if exercised, so holding is always at least as good, and there is no decision to make. The curve is only needed where exercising pays something, so Longstaff and Schwartz fit it there, and all three coefficients go to the share prices where the choice matters. Why keep the realized cash flows instead of the fitted values? After the decision, a path that holds keeps its realized Y i ( t ) Y_{i}(t) ; only a path that exercises changes its cash flow, to V ( K , S i ( t ) ) V(K,S_{i}(t)) . Suppose instead that each in-the-money path took the larger of V V and H ̂ t \\hat{H}_{t} . Wherever the curve came out too high, we would keep its value; wherever it came out too low, the payoff would replace it. The curve’s errors would no longer cancel, and the premium would drift up: on the eight paths, to 0.1178 USD/share instead of 0.1144. Keeping the realized cash flows uses the curve only to choose when to exercise. If we fit the curve on one set of paths and price on new paths, every cash flow is one a holder could actually receive, so a poor curve can only lower the price. On the same eight paths, the curve has partly seen each path’s future, so 0.1144 can still be a little high; with thousands of paths, as in Task 2 of the example, this effect is small. Discounting the date-1 cash flows to today and averaging gives the American premium, 0.1144 USD/share. Always holding on the same paths gives the European premium, 0.0564 USD/share, so the early-exercise premium is about 0.058 USD/share. Try it. Suppose a ninth path had S ( 2 ) = 0.90 S(2)=0.90 USD/share. Using the same fit, would it exercise at t = 2 t=2 ? Show the answer Yes. Exercising pays 1.10 − 0.90 = 0.20 1.10-0.90=0.20 USD/share, more than the fitted value of holding, H ̂ 2 ( 0.90 ) = − 1.070 + 2.983 ( 0.90 ) − 1.813 ( 0.81 ) = 0.146 U S D / s h a r e . \\hat{H}_{2}(0.90)=-1.070+2.983(0.90)-1.813(0.81)=0.146\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}. At each exercise date, we fit a curve to the in-the-money paths that estimates the value of holding from today's share price alone. A path exercises when exercising now pays more than that estimate. The curve is used only to make this choice; each path then keeps the cash flow it actually receives.", "url": "questions/lsm-regression.html"}, {"id": "exercised-but-profitable", "title": "How can a short option be exercised and still make money?", "theme": "strategies", "keywords": "POE POP probability of exercise probability of profit short call short put assigned assignment breakeven strike seller buyer exercised profit", "text": "Two reference points. In the L10a example, we sell an NVDA call with strike K = 235 K=235 USD/share and premium 𝒫 c = 9.88 \\mathcal{P}_{c}=9.88 USD/share, expiring in T = 0.1178 T=0.1178 year, with g y = 0.0413 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.0413\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . The call is exercised when S ( T ) > K + 0.01 S(T)>K+0.01 . The seller’s profit at expiration is the premium grown to expiration minus the payoff owed: P ̂ 𝒞 ( S ( T ) ) = 𝒟 T , 0 ( g y ) ⋅ 𝒫 c − ( S ( T ) − K ) + = 9.93 − ( S ( T ) − 235 ) + , \\hat{P}_{\\mathcal{C}}(S(T))=\\mathcal{D}_{T,0}(g_{y})\\cdot\\mathcal{P}_{c}-(S(T)-K)^{+}=9.93-(S(T)-235)^{+}, which is positive below the breakeven S ‡ = K + 𝒟 T , 0 ( g y ) ⋅ 𝒫 c = 244.93 S^{\\ddagger}=K+\\mathcal{D}_{T,0}(g_{y})\\cdot\\mathcal{P}_{c}=244.93 USD/share. So the share price at expiration falls into one of three ranges: Share price at expiration Exercised? Seller’s profit (USD/share) S ( T ) ≤ 235.01 S(T)\\leq 235.01 no 9.93, the whole premium 235.01 < S ( T ) < 244.93 235.01<S(T)<244.93 yes between 0 and 9.93 S ( T ) > 244.93 S(T)>244.93 yes negative, without limit At S ( T ) = 240 S(T)=240 USD/share, the call is exercised and the seller owes 240 − 235 = 5 240-235=5 USD/share, but keeps a profit of 9.93 − 5 = 4.93 9.93-5=4.93 USD/share. Being assigned means the seller must deliver the shares at the strike price; it does not mean the trade lost money. How do the probabilities fit together? The seller profits when the call is not exercised, or when it is exercised but finishes below the breakeven: seller’s POP = ( 1 − POE ) ⏟ S ( T ) ≤ K + 0.01 + ℙ ( K + 0.01 < S ( T ) < S ‡ ) ⏟ exercised, still profitable . \\text{seller's POP}=\\underbrace{(1-\\text{POE})}_{S(T)\\,\\leq\\,K+0.01}+\\underbrace{\\mathbb{P}\\left(K+0.01<S(T)<S^{\\ddagger}\\right)}_{\\text{exercised, still profitable}}. Under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} , the POE is 0.4295, so 0.6976 = 0.5705 + 0.1271 0.6976=0.5705+0.1271 : about one time in eight, the seller is assigned and still makes money. For the buyer, the same middle range is exercised but unprofitable, so the buyer’s POP is smaller than the POE. The same thing in a composite contract. In the L11a put credit spread on AMD, the short put at K 2 = 520 K_{2}=520 USD/share is exercised below 519.99 USD/share, but the breakeven is S ‡ = 511.20 S^{\\ddagger}=511.20 USD/share, below the strike by the credit grown to expiration. Under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} , the short put’s POE is 0.492 and the spread’s POP is 0.540, which is larger than 1 − 0.492 = 0.508 1-0.492=0.508 . Between 511.20 and 520 USD/share, the short put is assigned and the spread still makes money. Multi-leg breakevens shows how to find them for any composite contract. Try it. What is the seller’s profit on the NVDA call if S ( T ) = 250 S(T)=250 USD/share? Show the answer 9.93 − ( 250 − 235 ) = − 5.07 9.93-(250-235)=-5.07 USD/share. The share price is above the breakeven, so the payoff owed is larger than the premium kept. Exercise depends on the strike price, but profit depends on the breakeven, and the premium moves the breakeven past the strike. Between the two, the seller is assigned, but the premium still covers what the seller owes. So the seller's POP is larger than one minus the POE, and the buyer's POP is smaller than the POE.", "url": "questions/exercised-but-profitable.html"}, {"id": "composite-breakevens", "title": "How do we track the signs, breakevens, and POP of a multi-leg contract?", "theme": "strategies", "keywords": "composite contract premium sign debit credit theta orientation breakeven maximum loss iron condor credit spread POP multi-leg legs intervals strikes", "text": "Step 1: the composite premium and its sign. Each premium 𝒫 i \\mathcal{P}_{i} is a positive number, and the orientation θ i \\theta_{i} ( 1 1 bought, − 1 -1 sold) sets its sign. So the composite premium is what we pay minus what we collect: 𝒫 𝒞 = ∑ i ∈ 𝒞 θ i n i 𝒫 i = ∑ long legs n i 𝒫 i − ∑ short legs n i 𝒫 i . \\mathcal{P}_{\\mathcal{C}}=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{C}}\\theta_{i}\\,n_{i}\\,\\mathcal{P}_{i}=\\sum_{\\text{long legs}}n_{i}\\,\\mathcal{P}_{i}-\\sum_{\\text{short legs}}n_{i}\\,\\mathcal{P}_{i}. A positive 𝒫 𝒞 \\mathcal{P}_{\\mathcal{C}} is a price we pay, a debit. A negative one is a price we are paid, a credit of − 𝒫 𝒞 -\\mathcal{P}_{\\mathcal{C}} . Take an iron condor on a share at S ( 0 ) = 100 S(0)=100 USD/share, 30 days from expiration, with these quotes (European BSM premiums at σ = 0.30 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.30\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , in USD/share): a long put at 85 for 0.08, a short put at 90 for 0.40, a short call at 110 for 0.66, and a long call at 115 for 0.22. Then: 𝒫 𝒞 = ( 1 ) ( 0.08 ) + ( − 1 ) ( 0.40 ) + ( − 1 ) ( 0.66 ) + ( 1 ) ( 0.22 ) = − 0.76 U S D / s h a r e , \\begin{aligned} \\mathcal{P}_{\\mathcal{C}}&=(1)(0.08)+(-1)(0.40)+(-1)(0.66)+(1)(0.22)\\\\ &=-0.76\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}, \\end{aligned} a credit of 0.76 USD/share. With g y = 0.04 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.04\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , 𝒟 T , 0 ( g y ) = e 0.04 ⋅ 30 / 365 = 1.0033 \\mathcal{D}_{T,0}(g_{y})=e^{0.04\\cdot30/365}=1.0033 , so the credit grown to expiration is 0.7625 0.7625 USD/share. Step 2: the profit on each interval. Between neighboring strikes, each leg stays in or out of the money, so the profit is a straight line. On each interval, keep only the in-the-money legs. Below 85 USD/share, for example, both puts pay: P ̂ 𝒞 ( S ( T ) ) = 0.7625 − ( 90 − S ( T ) ) + ( 85 − S ( T ) ) = 0.7625 − 5 = − 4.2375 U S D / s h a r e . \\begin{aligned} \\hat{P}_{\\mathcal{C}}(S(T))&=0.7625-(90-S(T))+(85-S(T))\\\\ &=0.7625-5=-4.2375\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}. \\end{aligned} The other intervals follow the same way (USD/share): Interval In-the-money legs Profit at expiration S ( T ) < 85 S(T)<85 both puts − 4.2375 -4.2375 85 ≤ S ( T ) < 90 85\\leq S(T)<90 short put S ( T ) − 89.2375 S(T)-89.2375 90 ≤ S ( T ) ≤ 110 90\\leq S(T)\\leq110 none 0.7625 0.7625 110 < S ( T ) ≤ 115 110<S(T)\\leq115 short call 110.7625 − S ( T ) 110.7625-S(T) S ( T ) > 115 S(T)>115 both calls − 4.2375 -4.2375 Step 3: breakevens, maximum profit, and maximum loss. Setting each sloped line to zero gives S − ‡ = 90 − 0.7625 = 89.2375 S^{\\ddagger}_{-}=90-0.7625=89.2375 and S + ‡ = 110 + 0.7625 = 110.7625 S^{\\ddagger}_{+}=110+0.7625=110.7625 USD/share. The maximum profit is the credit grown to expiration, 0.7625 USD/share, and the maximum loss is 5 − 0.7625 = 4.2375 5-0.7625=4.2375 USD/share on either side. Why don’t the two sides’ losses add up? Each side does act as its own credit spread. As separate trades, the put spread collects 0.40 − 0.08 = 0.32 0.40-0.08=0.32 and the call spread 0.66 − 0.22 = 0.44 0.66-0.22=0.44 USD/share. At S ( T ) = 80 S(T)=80 USD/share, the put spread loses 5 USD/share less its credit grown to expiration, and the call spread keeps its credit grown to expiration. The two credits add up to the condor’s credit, so together they make the condor’s maximum loss: ( 0.32 + 0.44 ) ( 1.0033 ) − 5 = 0.7625 − 5 = − 4.2375 U S D / s h a r e . (0.32+0.44)(1.0033)-5=0.7625-5=-4.2375\\ \\mathrm{USD/share}. The share price can finish below 90 or above 110, but not both, so at most one side loses, and the other side’s credit always offsets part of it. Step 4: the probability of profit. The condor makes money between its breakevens, so POP = ℙ ( S − ‡ < S ( T ) < S + ‡ ) \\text{POP}=\\mathbb{P}\\left(S^{\\ddagger}_{-}<S(T)<S^{\\ddagger}_{+}\\right) . Under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} with the same σ = 0.30 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.30\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , the POP is 0.790. The short legs’ POEs are 0.111 (put) and 0.133 (call), and 1 − 0.111 − 0.133 = 0.756 1-0.111-0.133=0.756 is smaller than the POP: each breakeven lies beyond its short strike, so the condor still profits on some outcomes where a short leg is exercised ( exercised but profitable ). With simulated share prices, we skip the breakevens: compute the profit of each sample and count the positive ones. This works for any number of legs. Try it. Drop the long put, so the condor becomes a protected strangle. What happens to the credit, the lower breakeven, and the maximum loss below it? Show the answer The credit rises to 0.84 USD/share, or 0.8428 grown to expiration. The lower breakeven moves to 90 − 0.8428 = 89.1572 90-0.8428=89.1572 USD/share, and the maximum loss below it is 89.1572 USD/share, at S ( T ) = 0 S(T)=0 : the put side is no longer capped. Add the leg premiums with a plus sign for each leg we buy and a minus sign for each leg we sell. Then write the profit on each interval between the strikes, where it is a straight line, and set the sloped lines to zero to find the breakevens. The POP is the probability that the share price at expiration lands where the profit is positive.", "url": "questions/composite-breakevens.html"}, {"id": "closing-early", "title": "Why does closing a credit trade early earn less, and why do traders do it anyway?", "theme": "strategies", "keywords": "close early before expiration buy back extrinsic value time decay theta short strangle credit profit defend roll untested side 50% max profit gamma why wait", "text": "What does closing cost? To close a short leg at time t t , we buy it back at its current premium, its intrinsic value plus its extrinsic value E V ( t ) E_{V}(t) . So if we close a credit trade at a share price S S : profit if we close = credit grown to t ⏟ collected at entry − ( intrinsic value + E V ( t ) ) ⏟ cost to buy the legs back . \\text{profit if we close}=\\underbrace{\\text{credit grown to }t}_{\\text{collected at entry}}-\\underbrace{\\left(\\text{intrinsic value}+E_{V}(t)\\right)}_{\\text{cost to buy the legs back}}. At expiration, E V = 0 E_{V}=0 , and the profit at the same share price is the credit grown to T T minus the intrinsic value. The two differ by the extrinsic value still in the legs, plus a little interest on the credit. That is why, in the lecture’s figure, every curve before expiration lies below the profit at expiration. The lecture’s strangle in numbers. A put at K 1 = 90 K_{1}=90 and a call at K 2 = 110 K_{2}=110 USD/share are sold 30 days before expiration, with S ( 0 ) = 100 S(0)=100 USD/share, σ = 0.40 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.40\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , and g y = 0.04 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.04\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} . Priced on the CRR tree as American contracts, they bring in 1.00 and 1.44 USD/share, a credit of 2.44 USD/share. Repricing the legs at later dates gives the cost to buy both legs back and the profit if we close. The last column is the profit if we hold to expiration instead and the share price ends at the same value (USD/share): Days left Share price Cost to close Profit if closed If held 30 100 2.44 0.00 2.45 20 100 1.38 1.06 2.45 10 100 0.39 2.06 2.45 10 108 2.04 0.40 2.45 Both legs are out of the money in every row, so the whole cost to buy back is extrinsic value. To estimate it for a real trade, we read the current quotes for each leg, or reprice each leg on the CRR tree at the current share price, days left, and implied volatility, as here. Why close before expiration? “Closing early earns less” compares the two at the same share price. The share price at expiration is still unknown, and closing trades the profit we have for the profit we might have. What is left to earn: With 10 days left at S = 100 S=100 , closing locks in 2.06 of the 2.45 USD/share possible. Holding can add at most the 0.39 USD/share of extrinsic value left. What is left to lose: The call side still loses without limit above the upper breakeven, 110 + 2.45 = 112.45 110+2.45=112.45 USD/share. Near expiration, a small move near a strike also changes the profit by more, because the curves bend toward the kinks at the strikes. A move toward a strike makes this concrete: at S = 108 S=108 with 10 days left, the call has grown more expensive, and closing keeps only 0.40 USD/share. This is the reasoning behind rules such as tastylive’s in the lecture: close at half the maximum profit or about 21 days before expiration. If the share price moves toward a short strike, we can close and take the loss, or defend the trade. One defense from L11a is to roll the untested side: buy back the leg the share price has moved away from, and sell a new one closer to the share price for more credit. Either way, we weigh the credit left to earn against what the trade can still lose. Try it. With 20 days left and the share price still at 100 USD/share, what fraction of the credit do we keep by closing? Show the answer 1.06 / 2.44 ≈ 0.43 1.06/2.44\\approx0.43 , close to half, as the lecture’s right panel shows. Buying a short leg back costs its intrinsic value plus its remaining extrinsic value, so at any share price, closing early earns less than holding to expiration would at that same price. Traders close anyway because the extrinsic value left is all the trade can still earn, while the call side can still lose without limit.", "url": "questions/closing-early.html"}, {"id": "collar-strikes", "title": "How do a collar's strikes set its cost, floor, cap, and chance of loss?", "theme": "strategies", "keywords": "protective collar strike choice put call floor cap net debit credit zero-cost breakeven opportunity point O+ probability of loss assignment early exercise dividend covered call keep shares", "text": "What does each strike set? With shares bought at S ( 0 ) S(0) , a put bought at K 1 K_{1} for 𝒫 1 \\mathcal{P}_{1} , and a call sold at K 2 K_{2} for 𝒫 2 \\mathcal{P}_{2} , every number in L11b follows from one quantity, the total cost grown to expiration, C = 𝒟 T , 0 ( g y ) ⋅ ( S ( 0 ) + 𝒫 1 − 𝒫 2 ) C=\\mathcal{D}_{T,0}(g_{y})\\cdot\\left(S(0)+\\mathcal{P}_{1}-\\mathcal{P}_{2}\\right) : maximum loss = C − K 1 , maximum profit = K 2 − C , S ‡ = C . \\text{maximum loss}=C-K_{1},\\qquad\\text{maximum profit}=K_{2}-C,\\qquad S^{\\ddagger}=C. Raising K 1 K_{1} lifts the floor, but the put costs more, so C C rises. Raising K 2 K_{2} lifts the cap, but the call collects less, so C C also rises. The net premium 𝒫 1 − 𝒫 2 \\mathcal{P}_{1}-\\mathcal{P}_{2} decides whether the collar opens for a debit, at zero cost, or for a credit. Three collars on one share. Take S ( 0 ) = 100 S(0)=100 USD/share, three months to expiration, and g y = 0.04 y r − 1 g_{y}=0.04\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1} , so 𝒟 T , 0 ( g y ) = 1.0101 \\mathcal{D}_{T,0}(g_{y})=1.0101 . The quotes (USD/share) are BSM premiums at σ = 0.25 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.25\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} : puts at 95 for 2.43 and at 90 for 1.13, calls at 105 for 3.35 and at 110 for 1.92. Each collar is labeled by its strikes K 1 / K 2 K_{1}/K_{2} , amounts are in USD/share, and the last column is the probability of a loss under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} , ℚ ( S ( T ) < S ‡ ) \\mathbb{Q}(S(T)<S^{\\ddagger}) : Position Net premium Max loss Max profit Breakeven ℚ \\mathbb{Q} (loss) 95/105 credit 0.92 5.08 4.92 100.08 0.495 95/110 debit 0.51 6.52 8.48 101.52 0.541 90/110 credit 0.79 10.21 9.79 100.21 0.500 Shares 101.01 unlimited 101.01 0.525 For K 1 = 95 K_{1}=95 , a call near 108 USD/share would pay for the whole put, a zero-cost collar. Listed strikes come in steps, so in practice we pick the nearest one. Why can a collar lose more often than the shares? The 95/110 collar opens for a debit, so its breakeven, 101.52, sits above the shares’ 101.01. Between the strikes, it moves one for one with the shares but trails them by the debit grown to expiration, so it loses on slightly more outcomes, 0.541 against 0.525. In exchange, it can never lose more than 6.52 USD/share, while the shares can lose 101.01. The shares alone earn more for any S ( T ) > K 1 − 𝒟 T , 0 ( g y ) ⋅ ( 𝒫 1 − 𝒫 2 ) = 94.48 S(T)>K_{1}-\\mathcal{D}_{T,0}(g_{y})\\cdot(\\mathcal{P}_{1}-\\mathcal{P}_{2})=94.48 USD/share; below that, the floor pays. A credit collar such as 95/105 lowers the breakeven instead, but caps the profit sooner. Which strikes should we choose? No formula picks them. The put strike sets the largest loss we accept, the call strike the largest gain we give up, and the net premium follows from both. The SPY collar example compares these numbers using market quotes. Does a collar protect us from losing the shares? No. A collar holds the same short call as a covered call. If S ( T ) > K 2 S(T)>K_{2} , the call is exercised and the shares are sold at K 2 K_{2} . The put protects the value of the shares, not our ownership of them. Listed calls are American, so the shares can also be called away before expiration, most likely just before an ex-dividend date, when the call is deep in the money and the dividend is worth more than the extrinsic value left in the call (L10a). To keep the shares, we must buy the call back before then, at its current premium. Try it. For the 95/110 collar, what are the profits at S ( T ) = 80 S(T)=80 and S ( T ) = 120 S(T)=120 USD/share, and what would the shares alone earn? Show the answer The collar earns 95 − 101.52 = − 6.52 95-101.52=-6.52 and 110 − 101.52 = 8.48 110-101.52=8.48 USD/share. The shares alone earn 80 − 101.01 = − 21.01 80-101.01=-21.01 and 120 − 101.01 = 18.99 120-101.01=18.99 USD/share. A higher put strike raises the floor but costs more. A higher call strike raises the cap but brings in less. If the collar costs money to open, its breakeven sits above that of the shares, so it loses slightly more often than the shares, but never by more than its maximum loss. A collar still contains a sold call, so the shares can be called away just as in a covered call.", "url": "questions/collar-strikes.html"}, {"id": "net-delta", "title": "What does the net Delta of a covered call or collar tell us?", "theme": "strategies", "keywords": "net Delta covered call collar shares equivalent hedge sensitivity gamma expiration delta-neutral portfolio put call Delta two meanings", "text": "What does the number mean? For n n contracts on m = 100 m=100 shares each, L11b gives: Δ net = m n ( 1 − Δ c ) (covered call) Δ net = m n ( 1 + Δ p − Δ c ) (collar) \\begin{aligned} \\Delta_{\\text{net}}&=mn\\left(1-\\Delta_{c}\\right)&&\\text{(covered call)}\\\\ \\Delta_{\\text{net}}&=mn\\left(1+\\Delta_{p}-\\Delta_{c}\\right)&&\\text{(collar)} \\end{aligned} Its unit is shares: for a small move of Δ S \\Delta S USD/share, the position gains or loses about Δ net ⋅ Δ S \\Delta_{\\text{net}}\\cdot\\Delta S USD, as if it held Δ net \\Delta_{\\text{net}} shares. The shares contribute m n mn , the short call − m n Δ c -mn\\,\\Delta_{c} , and the long put m n Δ p mn\\,\\Delta_{p} . Because 0 ≤ Δ c ≤ 1 0\\leq\\Delta_{c}\\leq1 and − 1 ≤ Δ p ≤ 0 -1\\leq\\Delta_{p}\\leq0 , both options lower the net Delta: the short call gives up part of each rise, and the long put gains when the shares fall. One collar at entry. Take the 95/110 collar from the collar strikes answer , one contract on 100 shares at S = 100 S=100 USD/share with three months left, σ = 0.25 y r − 1 / 2 \\sigma=0.25\\,\\mathrm{yr}^{-1/2} , and BSM Deltas Δ c = 0.268 \\Delta_{c}=0.268 and Δ p = − 0.290 \\Delta_{p}=-0.290 : Δ net = 100 ( 1 − 0.268 ) = 73.2 (covered call) Δ net = 100 ( 1 − 0.290 − 0.268 ) = 44.2 (collar) \\begin{aligned} \\Delta_{\\text{net}}&=100\\,(1-0.268)=73.2&&\\text{(covered call)}\\\\ \\Delta_{\\text{net}}&=100\\,(1-0.290-0.268)=44.2&&\\text{(collar)} \\end{aligned} If the share price rises 2 USD/share, the 100 shares alone gain 200 USD, the covered call about 146 USD, and the collar about 88 USD. How does it change with the share price and time? Recomputing the BSM Deltas at other share prices gives the net Delta (shares): Share price 85 95 100 105 110 120 Covered call, 3 months left 97.3 84.9 73.2 59.1 44.3 20.1 Collar, 3 months left 20.0 40.5 44.2 41.8 34.9 17.9 Covered call, 5 days left 100.0 100.0 99.9 94.0 48.7 0.1 Collar, 5 days left 0.0 51.3 96.2 94.0 48.7 0.1 Near expiration, between the strikes, both options are almost worthless and barely move, so the collar behaves like the 100 shares. Beyond a strike, the deep in-the-money option cancels the shares: below 95 USD/share, the put gains what the shares lose, and above 110 USD/share, the call loses what the shares gain. The value is then locked at the floor or the cap, and the net Delta is close to zero. At a strike, the net Delta swings quickly between these values; Gamma measures how fast. Is this Delta a probability? Not for a position. A single call’s Delta is close to, and slightly above, its probability of exercise under ℚ \\mathbb{Q} ( why ); for a put the match is rougher. Each option’s Delta is also its sensitivity to the share price. The net Delta adds shares and option Deltas, so only the sensitivity meaning carries over: 44.2 is a number of shares, not a probability. Does a net Delta of zero mean no risk? No. Delta is the slope of the position’s value at today’s share price. Gamma changes it as the share price moves, and Theta, Vega, and Rho change the value through time, volatility, and the rate (L10a). For a large move, we reprice the legs instead of relying on Delta. Try it. With 5 days left at S = 105 S=105 USD/share, why do the covered call and the collar have the same net Delta, 94.0? Show the answer The put at 95 USD/share is far out of the money, so Δ p ≈ 0 \\Delta_{p}\\approx0 , and the collar behaves like the covered call. Its call at 110 USD/share still has a small Delta, 0.06. Net Delta is the number of shares the whole position behaves like for a small move in the share price. A covered call behaves like fewer shares than it holds, and a collar like fewer still. Near expiration, a collar behaves like the shares between its strikes and like no shares at all beyond them.", "url": "questions/net-delta.html"}];
